nt%20Lifecycle%20Policy%20Standard.pdf" rel=3D"nofollow">Group Information =
Risk Policy (GIRP)</a></p>
     </div></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <div class=3D"content-wrapper">
      <p>NA</p>
     </div></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <div class=3D"content-wrapper">
      <p>NA</p>
     </div></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <div class=3D"content-wrapper">
      <ul>
       <li>Systems &amp; Process Development Lifecycle Policy Standard - Gr=
oup Information Risk Policy</li>
      </ul>
     </div></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h2 class=3D"auto-cursor-target" id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-=
1.3GlossaryofTerms">1.3 Glossary of Terms</h2>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 73=
.1237%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 15.9795%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 84.0156%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Term</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Definition</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">PCI DSS</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">
     <p>A standard for handling<span>&nbsp;</span><em>Payment Card Industry=
<span>&nbsp;</span></em>data, particularly card numbers but also other key =
identifiers (such as expiry date, CVC). PCI requirements are spelt out it a=
 series of documents that provide requirements as to where and when PANs ma=
y be exposed and where they may not.&nbsp;</p>
     <p>Not only is the Bank responsible for managing our own issued cards =
according to the PCI standards, we are responsible for protecting other PCI=
 information that we may hold as a result of, for example, processing payme=
nts for our merchants.</p></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">PII</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">
     <p>Personally Identifiably Information. This is information which can =
be used to identify individuals, such as names, addresses, occupations, and=
 the like. PII does not have a single, clear definition, and can be highly =
contextual: it may be possible, for example, for information that does not =
appear to be PII in isolation to become PII in combination with other infor=
mation.</p>
     <p>When considering whether a data element is considered PII, or could=
 be considered PII, it can be helpful to consult with the Bank's privacy an=
d data owners to assess it.</p></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">PAN</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">The&nbsp;<em>Primary Account Number</em><span>&nbsp;</span>or&nbsp;<em>=
Payment Card Number</em><span>&nbsp;</span>that is the identifier of an iss=
ued credit card (or debit card). This is what most of us think of as our cr=
edit card number. The PCI DSS standards impose strict handling rules around=
 when these may be shown in the clear and when they must be obfuscated or t=
okenised in some way.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">BIN</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">
     <p>The&nbsp;<em>Bank Identification Number,&nbsp;</em><span>&nbsp;</sp=
an>which is a part of the PAN which identifies a given bank (or other card =
issuer). For example, BNZ BINs include the 4506 and 4999 prefix.</p>
     <p>BINs are publicly listed, and, while a part of PCI, are not in and =
of themselves confidential.</p></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">SoR</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">
     <p>This refers to the System of Record, which in the case of this desi=
gn is the True Source System feeding Snowflake.</p></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h1 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-2.Overview">2. Overview</h1>
<p>This document outlines how data classifications identified in Alation, t=
hrough scans of Snowflake environments, will be used to apply and enforce c=
lassifications within Snowflake. Classifications defined in Alation (e.g. <=
em>Public, Confidential, Highly Confidential</em>) will be synchronized and=
 applied as tags in Snowflake, driving dynamic masking policies that contro=
l data visibility for different user roles and personas.</p>
<p>This design ensures a consistent, automated link between metadata govern=
ance in Alation and data access enforcement in Snowflake, enabling secure, =
role-based access to data across all environments.</p>
<h1 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-3.Objectives">3. <span class=
=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=3D"8b8cf9d6-d7b4-4dc7-86dc-75d9c7d54497=
">Objec</span>tives</h1>
<p>This document defines the end-to-end design for the Snowflake Classifica=
tion &amp; Masking Solution and how Alation will be used to automate and co=
ntrol data masking within Snowflake environments. Specifically, this design=
 outlines how metadata captured in Alation is propagated, transformed, and =
applied in Snowflake to enforce consistent, role-based access to sensitive =
data.</p>
<p>The key objectives are to:</p>
<ul>
 <li>Align Alation classifications with Snowflake masking logic=20
  <ul>
   <li>Show how classifications (e.g., <em>Highly Confidential</em>, <em>Co=
nfidential</em>, <em>Public</em>) flow from Alation through Alation Analyti=
cs to Snowflake.</li>
   <li>Explain how classifications are applied as Snowflake Tags, driving d=
ynamic masking and role-based access controls.</li>
  </ul></li>
 <li>Detail the Integration Components=20
  <ul>
   <li>Briefly describe how Alation scans Snowflake environments using Cata=
logue Sets and Data Sources.</li>
   <li>Outline the roles of<strong> </strong>Alation Analytics, Secure Data=
 Shares, and the Governance Database in transferring and managing classific=
ations.</li>
   <li>Explain how classifications are synchronised across environments dur=
ing code promotion.</li>
  </ul></li>
 <li>Support scalability and automation=20
  <ul>
   <li>Enable automated daily refreshes and classifications in alignment wi=
th broader governance processes.</li>
   <li>Provide a scalable foundation for compliance validation and future e=
xtensions (e.g.. integration of PROD data into NON-PROD environments).</li>
  </ul></li>
</ul>
<h1 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-4.Scope">4. Scope</h1>
<h2 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-4.1OpenItemsForNextDesignIter=
ation">4.1 Open Items For Next Design Iteration</h2>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 95=
.3195%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 5.09137%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 9.8056%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 85.0919%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Item</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Category</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Description</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td style=3D"text-align: center;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluen=
ceTd">1</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">Production Data to Non Production Environments</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">The following design provides the foundation for managing the integrati=
on of PROD data into lower environments. To enable this, further enhancemen=
ts to the design will be required to ensure this solution to can securely m=
anage the handling of PROD data into lower environments.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td style=3D"text-align: center;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluen=
ceTd">2</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">Scanning of Inbound Snowflake Data Shares</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">
     <p>In context of this solution, scanning of the inbound data share fro=
m Alation Analytics to Snowflake is considered low risk as the data share <=
span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=3D"8a947cad-017f-474b-920c-c1=
82bea39f97">constitutes sharing of metadata only (e.g. classification of da=
ta fields)</span>. Actual data values for any given field will not be avail=
able within Alation and by extension not included in the data share from Al=
ation Analytics. Any erroneous or extraneous data included within this shar=
e is considered low risk. In context of future use cases outside of the Ala=
tion data share, the primary objective is to establish a repeatable pattern=
 for scanning all inbound data shares, especially where actual data values =
are shared (e.g. the name of a person). Subsequently, there is a request to=
 leverage this design as an opportunity define a repeatable pattern for fut=
ure inbound data shares.&nbsp;</p></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td style=3D"text-align: center;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluen=
ceTd">3</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">Use of Snowflake NON-PROD environment</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">
     <p>It was raised that Snowflake NON-PROD and integration against Alati=
on Analytics may need to be included as part of this design. Historically, =
Snowflake NON-PROD has not been considered as an in-scope environment as pa=
rt of the BDH Uplift Program. Subsequently,&nbsp; a wider platform pattern =
is in the process of being established, which will be used to evaluate what=
 design items will need to include the use of Snowflake NON-PROD based on a=
 define set of criteria. If it is deemed that Snowflake NON-PROD must inclu=
ded as part of this design, then this paper will need to be adjusted accord=
ingly, however it should not fundamentally changed the core design.</p></td=
>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h2 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-4.2InScopeFor">4.2 In <span c=
lass=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=3D"e2f24cbb-8ed8-4a02-9f6f-c86c8cff=
212f">Scop</span>e For&nbsp;</h2>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 95=
.3195%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 5.08363%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 9.8255%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 85.1189%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Item</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Category</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Description</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td style=3D"text-align: center;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluen=
ceTd">1</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">Classification &amp; Masking Entry Point</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td"><span>This solution includes the masking of data fields in Snowflake ba=
sed on Alation classifications that have been set and specified by Alation.=
 The application of masking logic will commence from the point at which the=
 tag values are made available to Snowflake via a secure data share. This h=
andoff represents the entry point into the scope of this solution.&nbsp;</s=
pan></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h2 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-4.3OutofScope">4.3 Out of Sco=
pe</h2>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 95=
.4009%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 5.12465%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 9.95037%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 84.9403%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Item</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Category</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Description</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td style=3D"text-align: center;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluen=
ceTd">1</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">PCI Data</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">Any&nbsp;<span style=3D"color:#172b4d;">tokenization and/or obfuscation=
 of PCI data (e.g. credit cards) is outside the scope of this solution; it =
is assumed that all tokenization of such data should be executed further up=
stream prior to provisioning of tokens into Snowflake BDH.</span></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td style=3D"text-align: center;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluen=
ceTd">2</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">Alation Application Deployments</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">
     <p><span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=3D"cf6f022b-7ec4-43a=
c-9330-d53c67619570">Detailed design activities related to the development,=
 configuration or processes facilitating deployments of objects (e.g. Alati=
on Data Sources or Alation Catalogue Sets) used for Alation PRE-PROD or PRO=
D environments is out of scope for this solution; any specific Alation desi=
gn and environment configuration decisions will need to be captured and rev=
ised as part of the </span><a href=3D"https://confluence.nz.thenational.com=
/display/ECPKB/Alation+Phase+1+Detailed+Design" rel=3D"nofollow"><span clas=
s=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=3D"cf6f022b-7ec4-43ac-9330-d53c6761957=
0">Alation Phase 1 - Detailed Design&nbsp;</span></a>Note: although code pr=
omotion process will need to be documented in a separate design, this page =
does briefly describe the intended components to provide context of the end=
-end view of the solution.&nbsp;</p></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td style=3D"text-align: center;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluen=
ceTd">3</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">Classification Rules</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td"><span>The determination and definition of classification rules for data=
 attributes are outside the scope of this solution. These rules will be est=
ablished by Alation in collaboration with the designated data steward and D=
ata Management &amp; Governance (DM&amp;G) team. Snowflake's role in this p=
rocess is limited to receiving the final classifications and applying appro=
priate data masking based on those classifications. <span>It is important t=
o note that Alation=E2=80=99s classification logic is based solely on metad=
ata and not on underlying data values. There are ongoing initiatives being =
explored to enhance this capability by integrating SecurDPS as a mechanism =
to validate the accuracy of Alation=E2=80=99s metadata-based classification=
s against the actual data values.</span></span></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td style=3D"text-align: center;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluen=
ceTd">4</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">Propagation of Data Classification from SoR to Warehouses</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">
     <p><span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=3D"201ace58-7d03-447=
f-aac5-6eb7f1b6d6f9">The automatic propagation of data classifications from=
 a source system of record (SoR) to the corresponding scanned representatio=
n of the same field in Snowflake, following an Alation scan, is out of scop=
e for this solution. Although the Landing Layer will be scanned, a</span><s=
pan><span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=3D"201ace58-7d03-447f-aa=
c5-6eb7f1b6d6f9">ny classifications that are available within Alation for a=
 given SoR, will not be automatically inheriting the classification directl=
y from a scanned SoR within Alation; this is because the data structures re=
flected in the true SoR may be vastly different to the data that is actuall=
y ingested into Snowflake</span>. Instead, to ensure consistency the same '=
Catalogue Set' within Alation that was used to determine the classification=
s for a SoR, will be re-used to scan and apply classifications within Snowf=
lake. Effectively</span><span>, Snowflake is treated like any other SoR wit=
h its own set of classifications that are determined by a common set of rul=
es as defined in the 'Catalogue Sets'. This is documented in <a href=3D"#BD=
HClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-section_9.2">Appendix Item 2 - How Alatio=
n Scans and Applies Classifications</a>.</span></p></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td style=3D"text-align: center;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluen=
ceTd">5</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">Governance By Design Controls</td>
    <td style=3D"text-align: left;vertical-align: top;" class=3D"confluence=
Td">
     <p>Solutioning of other 'data governance by design' controls is out of=
 scope including: sensitive data usage monitoring, job event/activity logs =
for data transport and processing, job activity logs for changes to code an=
d configuration and data retention and disposal schedules.</p></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h1 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-5.Requirements">5. Requiremen=
ts</h1>
<h2 class=3D"auto-cursor-target" id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-=
5.1RegulatoryRequirements">5.1 Regulatory Requirements&nbsp;</h2>
<p><span>For completeness of the BDH Classification &amp; Masking solution,=
 the Data Management &amp; Governance (DM&amp;G) requirements are included =
in this document. These requirements are <u>primarily addressed upstream wi=
thin Alation</u>, where governance and classification logic are defined. Si=
nce the same classifications are subsequently applied within Snowflake, the=
 classification and masking within Snowflake effectively serves as an exten=
sion of Alation=E2=80=99s classification framework.&nbsp;</span></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"wrapped relative-table confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 95=
.8079%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 1.52995%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 73.1833%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 13.5146%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 11.7722%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">ID</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Requirement</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Reference / Source of Requirem=
ent</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Relevant BNZ Obligations</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">1</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">BNZ must classify data by criticality and se=
nsitivity taking into account how it will be used throughout its lifecycle,=
 ensuring the appropriate strength of data controls are applied.</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">APRA CPG 235</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">OBGI-005755 CPG 235 Data Classification and =
Access</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">2</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">BNZ must desensitise data to maintain its co=
nfidentiality when extending its usage.</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">APRA CPG 235</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">MOBG-009437 - (CPG 235) Data Classification =
and Access</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">3</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">The desensitisation strength for data should=
 take into account the ability to reconstruct the original data set using t=
he other data available.</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">APRA CPG 235</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">4</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">PII, PCI, PHI and common confidential and hi=
ghly confidential fields must have a valid business need to be consumed (Ac=
cess to data is only granted where required to conduct business processes).=
</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">APRA CPG 235</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">5</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Data Owners, or anyone deemed appropriate to=
 delegate the task to, must approve access to Confidential and Highly Confi=
dential data.</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">APRA CPG 235</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">6</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">BNZ must classify its information assets (in=
formation and information technology, including software, hardware and data=
, both soft and hard copy), including those managed by related parties and =
third parties, by criticality and sensitivity.&nbsp;</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">APRA CPS 234</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">MOBG-009130 - (CPS 234) Information classifi=
cation</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">7</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">All information asset criticality and sensit=
ivity classifications for BNZ must reflect the degree to which an informati=
on security incident affecting an information asset has the potential to af=
fect, financially or non-financially, the entity or the interests of deposi=
tors, policyholders, beneficiaries or other customers.</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">APRA CPS 234</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">MOBG-009130 - (CPS 234) Information classifi=
cation</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">8</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">BNZ should establish a centralised repositor=
y in the data catalog outlining the data treatments for data elements.</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">9</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Highly Confidential or Confidential Producti=
on data should not be stored or used in a non-production environment unless=
 either the data is desensitised (masked).</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">BNZ GIRP Systems and Process Development Lif=
ecycle Policy</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">10</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">BNZ Business Leaders must dispose of, or per=
manently deidentify personal information when it is no longer required for =
a permitted business use or overriding legal obligation, in accordance with=
 the Records Management Policy Standard and Record Retention Schedule. This=
 could be triggered by review cycles, legislation changes, data product ret=
irement, etc.</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">BNZ Privacy Policy Standard</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">11</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Special categories of personal information m=
ust be managed at a higher level of privacy protection than other personal =
information.</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">BNZ Privacy Policy Standard</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">12</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Access to special categories of personal inf=
ormation must be restricted to only those who require access for a legitima=
te business purpose.</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">BNZ Privacy Policy Standard</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">13</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">BNZ's card number masking/truncation standar=
d can be used to protect displayed, stored or printed card numbers. The sta=
ndard requires replacing card number digits, excluding the last 4 digits, w=
ith an =E2=80=9CX=E2=80=9D i.e. XXXX XXXX XXXX 1234. Any proposed variation=
 to the masking\truncation standard must be endorsed by the Product Card Pr=
otect Team.</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">BNZ Protecting Highly Confidential Payment C=
ard Data Guidance</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">14</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Electronic information in-transit must be pr=
otected from corruption, commensurate with the Integrity rating of the info=
rmation.</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Cryptography Policy Standard, BNZ Access Con=
trols Policy Standard</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">15</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">All Information Assets must be classified by=
 rating the potential impact of loss of Confidentiality, Integrity or Avail=
ability of information in accordance with NAB=E2=80=99s classification sche=
dules (See Annexure 1 Information Classification and Handling SOP).</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">GIRP - Information Classification and Handli=
ng Policy Standard</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">16</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Cardholder authentication data (such as full=
 track /chip data, PIN/PIN Block and card verification code or value) must =
not be stored post-authorisation.&nbsp;</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">GIRP - Information Classification and Handli=
ng Policy Standard</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">17</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Primary account numbers (PANs) must be unrea=
dable when displayed on computer screens or printed on reports, receipts or=
 faxes, by truncating the PAN (which is concealing a segment of PAN data so=
 that no more than the first six and last four digits of the PAN is display=
ed).&nbsp; Where there is a business need for full PANs to be readable, PAN=
s must be accessed within a secure area. (See GIRP Guidance) to know what c=
onstitutes as =E2=80=98Secure Area=E2=80=99.)&nbsp;</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">GIRP - Information Classification and Handli=
ng Policy Standard</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">18</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Primary account numbers (PANs) must be indec=
ipherable when they are stored by one or more of the following methods: <br=
>
      a. Hashing- irreversibly converting PAN data into a fixed length mess=
age digest using Cryptography. (See to the Cryptography Policy Standard for=
 guidance on encryption).<br>
      b. Tokenization- replacing a PAN with a cryptographic token that work=
s with an authentication server to perform dynamic or two-factor authentica=
tion.<br>
      c. Truncation - permanently removing a segment of PAN data so that no=
 more than the first six and last four digits of the PAN is stored.<br>
      d. Encryption - converting information into an unintelligible form ex=
cept to holders of a specific cryptographic key.</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">PCI DSS - 3.5<br>
      GIRP - Information Classification and Handling Policy Standard</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">19</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Government identifiers (e.g. Medicare, Tax F=
ile Numbers) must be handled in accordance with legislative requirements ap=
plicable to Personal Information, and must not be used to identify a custom=
er. See Privacy Policy Standard. (For more information see GIRP Guidance.).=
</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">GIRP - Information Classification and Handli=
ng Policy Standard</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">20</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Encryption schemes or solutions must be conf=
igured to have at least 128 bits of strength.</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">NAB Cryptographic Standards June 2024</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">21</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">PCI DSS requirements only apply to entities =
with environments where account data (card holder data and/or sensitive aut=
h data) is stored, processed, or transmitted and entities with environments=
 that can impact the security of cardholder data. These requirements also a=
pply to any entities that outsource payment operations or management of the=
ir cardholder data environment.&nbsp;</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">PCI DSS</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">22</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">If sensitive authentication data (SAD) is re=
ceived, examine documented policies, procedures, and system configurations =
to verify the data is not stored after authorisation.<br>
      - SAD is not stored after authorisation, even if encrypted. All sensi=
tive data received is rendered unrecoverable upon completion of the authori=
sation process<br>
      - The card verification code is not stored upon completion of the aut=
horisation process<br>
      - The personal identification number (PIN) and the PIN block are not =
stored upon completion of the authorisation process<br>
      - SAD that is stored electronically prior to completion of authorisat=
ion is encrypted using strong cryptography</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">PCI DSS - 3.3</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">OBGI-005178 CPS 234 Information Classificati=
on</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">23</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Primary account number (PAN) is secured wher=
ever it is stored and access to displays of full PAN and ability to copy PA=
N are restricted, including:<br>
      -PAN is indecipherable when displayed (the BIN and last four digits a=
re the maximum number of digits to be displayed), such that only personnel =
with a legitimate business need can see more than the BIN and last four dig=
its of the PAN<br>
      -When using remote-access technologies, technical controls prevent co=
py and/or relocation of PAN for all personnel, except for those with docume=
nted, explicit authorisation and a legitimate, defined business need</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">PCI DSS - 3.4</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">24</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">If personal information is held then the dat=
a must be protected by security safeguards via access controls, data obfusc=
ation and infrastructure security against loss, access, use/modification an=
d misuse.</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Privacy Act 2020</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">N/A</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">25</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">BNZ must regularly assess data quality and e=
valuate the effectiveness of data risk management and make any necessary ad=
justments to ensure identified control gaps are treated in a timely and sys=
tematic manner.&nbsp;</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">APRA CPG 235</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">MOBG-009444: Data Risk Metrics &amp; Reporti=
ng</td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h1 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-6.KeyDesignDecisions">6. Key =
Design Decisions</h1>
<p><span>The following section documents the list of key design decisions a=
nd the outcome of the decision that were addressed as part of the solutioni=
ng process. These key design decisions have been recorded upfront as they i=
mpact the solution design as documented in subsequent sections. Each decisi=
on has been evaluated to support informed decision-making as part of the br=
oader context of this document, ensuring alignment with strategic goals and=
 implementation requirements.</span></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 95=
.7632%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 9.37736%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 23.1487%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 67.4632%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Key Design Decision</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Name</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Description</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">KDD01</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Alation - Data Classification Options &amp; =
Role of Alation</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Alation will scan lower environments within =
Snowflake to provide classification instructions early in the data lifecycl=
e of a Data Product. Subsequently, classification against tables will not b=
e set by data engineers directly via dbt or as part of data engineering act=
ivities. For more information refer to: <a href=3D"#BDHClassification&amp;M=
askingDesign-section_9.1">Appendix Item 1 - KDD01 Data Classification Optio=
ns and Role of Alation</a></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">KDD02</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Alation - Integration to Alation Analytics</=
td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>Alation Analytics is the preferred integration pattern to extract c=
lassifications from Alation given the native Snowflake data sharing functio=
nality. This integration provides a performant, secure, and operationally l=
ightweight mechanism for data exchange. The secure data sharing model elimi=
nates the need for complex API management, external connectors or intermedi=
ary data movements.</p></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">KDD03</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Alation - Scanning of Selected Environments<=
/td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p><span>Snowflake SYST will not be scanned given the same classificat=
ions that are derived from scanning Snowflake DEV will be applied Snowflake=
 SYST; these will be used to inform masking rules in both these environment=
s. This same pattern applies to higher environments where classifications t=
hat are derived from scanning Snowflake PPTE will be applied Snowflake PROD=
 to inform masking rules in both these environments. Snowflake PROD will on=
ly be scanned for compliance purposes.</span></p></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-r=
ef=3D"1ecc8539-4335-496e-9037-e5b27e30a5f6">KDD04</span></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Landing Zone - To be Scanned By Alation</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>In addition to the Raw, Foundation and Curated data layers, a decis=
ion has been made to scan the Landing layer using Alation. This will ensure=
 early visibility and better classification of data earlier in the data pro=
duct lifecycle.</p></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">KDD05</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Lading Zone - Highly Confidential Role</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>For the Landing Engineer to complete their work they will need the =
ability to access data in the clear and to be able to see data in the clear=
 to validate the output. This is also required where complex data structure=
s (e.g. JSON) will need to be flatted. As a result, the following roles are=
 required:&nbsp;</p>
     <ul>
      <li style=3D"list-style-type: none;">
       <ul>
        <li style=3D"list-style-type: none;">
         <ul>
          <li>1 Role to Access Highly Confidential Data. By default any dat=
a landed will be Unclassified and treated as Highly Confidential initially.=
</li>
         </ul></li>
       </ul></li>
     </ul></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">KDD06</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Subject Area Zone - Available Roles</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>As a result of Decision KDD04, 3 roles will existing in the subject=
 area zone.&nbsp; These are:</p>
     <ul>
      <li style=3D"list-style-type: none;">
       <ul>
        <li style=3D"list-style-type: none;">
         <ul>
          <li>1 Role to Access Highly Confidential Data =E2=80=93 by defaul=
t, any data that is Unclassified will be treated as as Highly Confidential =
initially.</li>
          <li>1 Role to access Confidential Data =E2=80=93 once classified =
by Alation.</li>
          <li>1 Role to access data that is Private =E2=80=93 once classifi=
ed by Alation.</li>
         </ul></li>
       </ul></li>
     </ul></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">KDD07</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Masking Rules</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>Primary application of masking will be determined by the SECURITY_C=
LASSIFICATION_CODE<strong> </strong>only. No other classifications should b=
e used to inform masking. All other flags are required for GIRP compliance =
only. There are 3 values within this field that will drive masking, these a=
re are:</p>
     <ul>
      <ol>
       <li>Highly Confidential</li>
       <li>Confidential</li>
       <li>Private</li>
      </ol>
     </ul></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-r=
ef=3D"bf64fb7d-2e1e-4c35-a661-ffd349f52d49">KDD08</span></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Management of Masked Keys</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>The management of Business Keys (BKs), Primary Keys (PKs) and Forei=
gn Keys (FKs) within the Snowflake platform requires deliberate design to b=
alance data security, classification compliance, and developer and consumer=
 productivity. However, when these keys are classified as Highly Confidenti=
al, Confidential, Private or Unclassified, the data masking policies descri=
bed in this document are automatically applied; this may hinder productivit=
y A design decision was made to hash the&nbsp; BKs, PKs and FKs based on th=
eir data classification level to ensure these keys are exempt from masking;=
 this will still protect the data and prevent the values from being shown i=
n the clear but still enable functional usage of the data, e.g. joins acros=
s tables as the integrity of the day is retained.</p></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">KDD09</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Integration to Alation Analytics</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>Alation Analytics is the preferred integration pattern to extract c=
lassifications from Alation given the native Snowflake Data Sharing functio=
nality. This integration provides a performant, secure, and operationally l=
ightweight mechanism for data exchange. The secure data share model elimina=
tes the need for complex API management, external connectors or intermediar=
y data movements.</p></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h1 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-7.High-LevelSolutionDesign">7=
. High-Level Solution Design</h1>
<h2 class=3D"auto-cursor-target" id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-=
7.1HighLevelSolutionDiagram">7.1 High Level Solution Diagram</h2>
<p><span>The following section outlines the high-level solution, which has =
been shaped by the key design decisions detailed in subsequent section. A S=
olution Diagram is provided to illustrate the solution. Each&nbsp;<strong>K=
ey Component</strong> within the solution is annotated with a number. These=
 components will be referenced throughout this document.</span></p>
<p><map id=3D"gliffy-map-1968704278-6030" name=3D"gliffy-map-1968704278-603=
0"></map></p>
<table width=3D"100%" class=3D"gliffy-macro-table">
 <tbody>
  <tr>
   <td>
    <table class=3D"gliffy-macro-inner-table">
     <caption align=3D"bottom">
     </caption>
     <tbody>
      <tr>
       <td><img style=3D"border: none; width: 1200px;" usemap=3D"#gliffy-ma=
p-1968704278-6030" src=3D"9244fe3fc51b4b4825ef13358a8a3730a0dbc091ba9db289a=
c224dee25a76ebe" alt=3D"" class=3D"gliffy-macro-image" width=3D"468" height=
=3D"350"></td>
      </tr>
     </tbody>
    </table></td>
  </tr>
 </tbody>
</table>
<p></p>
<h2 class=3D"auto-cursor-target" id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-=
7.2HighLevelSolution-OverviewOfKeyArchitecturalComponents">7.2 High Level S=
olution - Overview Of Key Architectural Components<br><br></h2>
<div role=3D"region" aria-label=3D"Info" class=3D"confluence-information-ma=
cro  confluence-information-macro-information">
 <p class=3D"title conf-macro-render">Key Components Overview</p><span role=
=3D"presentation" class=3D"aui-icon aui-icon-small aui-iconfont-info-filled=
 confluence-information-macro-icon"></span>
 <div class=3D"confluence-information-macro-body">
  <p><span>The following table provides an overview of the solution, outlin=
ing each key component and its purpose. It is intended to give readers a co=
ncise understanding of how the overall classification and masking process w=
ill work, with consideration given to the different environments. The detai=
led design, logic, and implementation approach for each key component are d=
escribed in the subsequent sections of this document.</span></p>
 </div>
</div>
<p><br></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 96=
.8142%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 15.3276%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 26.6149%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 13.8525%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 44.1836%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Component Number</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Component Name</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Target Environment</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Purpose</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">
     <div class=3D"content-wrapper" title=3D"">
      <p>Component 1</p>
     </div></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Alation Scan of Snowflake DEV Environment</t=
d>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Snowflake DEV Database<br><br></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Alation scans selected Snowflake DEV databas=
es and data layers. Using the defined Catalogue Set, classifications are au=
tomatically assigned to fields storing the classification within Alation as=
 a custom field.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">Component 2</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Integration Between Alation &amp; Alation An=
alytics</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Alation Single Instance</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Alation classifications are propagated to Al=
ation Analytics via a scheduled ETL process that runs every 24 hours, ensur=
ing metadata remains current.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">Component 3</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>Integration Between Alation Analytics and Snowflake</p></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Snowflake PRE-PROD Account</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Classifications from Alation Analytics are s=
ecurely shared with the Snowflake PRE-PROD account through a Snowflake secu=
re data share.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">Component 4</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Use Of Governance Database To Store Classifi=
cations</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Snowflake PRE-PROD Account</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Classifications received from Alation Analyt=
ics are transformed and integrated into the within the Governance Database =
on the Snowflake Pre-Prod Account.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">Component 5</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Application of Classifications to Snowflake =
Tables</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Snowflake DEV Database</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">For scanned DEV databases, classifications i=
n the Governance Database are applied to Snowflake DEV tables as Snowflake =
Tags, defining data sensitivity and masking behavior.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">Component 6</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Role-Based Access and Masking Of Data</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Snowflake DEV Database</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Based on applied tags (e.g.&nbsp;Highly Conf=
idential, Confidential), Snowflake dynamically masks or unmasks fields depe=
nding on the user=E2=80=99s assigned role and access privileges.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">Component 7</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Role-Based Access and Masking Of Data</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Snowflake SYST Database</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">When Snowflake code is promoted from DEV to =
SYST, the same classifications are automatically re-applied, ensuring consi=
stent masking and data protection across environments.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">Component 8</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Promotion of Alation Configuration To Higher=
 Environments</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Alation Single Instance</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">To enable scanning and classification of hig=
her environments, Alation Catalog Sets and Data Source configurations are p=
romoted through a controlled process, ensuring consistent configuration are=
 applied to Snowflake PROD environments.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">Component 9</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Alation Scan of Snowflake PPTE Environment</=
td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Snowflake PPTE Database</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Using the Production Alation configurations,=
 Alation scans the Snowflake PPTE environment to identify and assign auto c=
lassifications to fields and tables.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">Component 10</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Integration Between Alation &amp; Alation An=
alytics</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Alation Single Instance</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Classifications for Snowflake PPTE are refre=
shed in Alation Analytics via the same 24-hour ETL process.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">Component 11</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Integration Between Alation Analytics and Sn=
owflake</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Snowflake PROD Account</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">The Alation Analytics classifications for Sn=
owflake PPTE are securely shared with the Snowflake PROD account using a Sn=
owflake secure data share.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">Component 12</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Use Of Governance Database To Store Classifi=
cations</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Snowflake PPTE Database</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Classifications shared from Alation Analytic=
s are transformed and integrated into the Governance Database within the Sn=
owflake PROD account, similar to the PRE-PROD process.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">Component 13</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Application of Classifications to Snowflake =
Tables</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Snowflake PPTE Database</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">For scanned PPTE databases, the integrated c=
lassifications are applied to Snowflake tables as tags, controlling how and=
 when data is masked.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">Component 14</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Role-Based Access and Masking Of Data</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Snowflake PPTE Database</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Tagged columns (e.g.&nbsp;Highly Confidentia=
l, Confidential) are dynamically masked or unmasked based on the Snowflake =
role accessing the data, ensuring consistent governance in PPTE.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">Component 15</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Role-Based Access and Masking Of Data</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Snowflake PROD Database</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">When code is promoted from Snowflake PPTE to=
 Snowflake PROD, the same classification tags are re-applied automatically,=
 maintaining end-to-end consistency and security.</td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#f4f5f7 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
f4f5f7" style=3D"text-align: center;" title=3D"Background color : Light gre=
y 100%">Component 16</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Compliance Scanning of Snowflake PROD</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Snowflake PROD Database</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Alation performs ongoing scans of Snowflake =
PROD for compliance monitoring and governance validation but does not alter=
 or redefine production tags or classifications.</td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h1 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.High-LevelSolutionDesign-De=
tailedArchitectureComponents">8. High-Level Solution Design - Detailed Arch=
itecture Components</h1>
<h2 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.1AlationScanofSnowflakeDeve=
lopmentEnvironment(Components1,8,9,16)">8.1 Alation Scan of Snowflake Devel=
opment Environment (Components 1, 8, 9, 16)</h2>
<div role=3D"region" aria-label=3D"Info" class=3D"confluence-information-ma=
cro  confluence-information-macro-information">
 <span role=3D"presentation" class=3D"aui-icon aui-icon-small aui-iconfont-=
info-filled confluence-information-macro-icon"></span>
 <div class=3D"confluence-information-macro-body">
  <p><span>The following section outlines the processes and activities that=
 occur within the Alation environment. While this is out of scope for the d=
etailed design of this solution, it is included to provide context of the e=
nd-to-end integration and illustrate how Alation interacts with Snowflake e=
nvironments as part of the broader solution framework.</span></p>
 </div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.1.1UseofAlationDataSources&=
amp;CatalogSets">8.1.1 Use of Alation Data Sources &amp; Catalog Sets</h3>
<p><span class=3D"doc">The fundamental unit of the Alation data catalog is =
the&nbsp;catalog page. Catalog pages hold information about a company=E2=80=
=99s data assets, including data sources, columns, policies, and more. For =
each data asset, the catalog page includes a title, description, and fields=
 for additional information.&nbsp;</span><span class=3D"doc">Catalog pages&=
nbsp;</span>of Alation objects are built on templates.</p>
<p>&nbsp;A&nbsp;template&nbsp;is a set of fields that are assembled into a =
page to deliver relevant information about a catalog object. For example, a=
 column object template might include fields for the column title, data typ=
e, and description.&nbsp;</p>
<p>Alation has three basic types of fields that can appear on a catalog pag=
e template:</p>
<ul class=3D"simple">
 <li>
  <p>Built-in fields&nbsp;are editable fields provided by Alation. Examples=
 of built-in fields include&nbsp;Title,&nbsp;Description, and&nbsp;Stewards=
.</p></li>
 <li>
  <p>Object-defined fields&nbsp;are read-only fields that are associated wi=
th a particular object type. Examples include&nbsp;Sample Columns,&nbsp;Tab=
le Constraint, and&nbsp;Data Type.</p></li>
 <li>
  <p><span class=3D"std std-ref">Custom fields</span> are user-defined fiel=
ds that can be added to templates. <u>Custom fields should be used to displ=
ay the classifications for the columns consistently across all data sources=
 including Snowflake.</u><strong>&nbsp;</strong>This is achieved using Cata=
log Sets.&nbsp;</p></li>
</ul>
<p><span style=3D"color:#333333;"><strong>Catalog Sets</strong></span></p>
<p>In Alation, catalog sets enable you to group pieces of data together to =
perform bulk operations on identical or similar data sets. Bulk edits are d=
one on the page of a catalog set, rather than on the individual pages of sp=
ecific data objects, one by one. In Alation, you can create two types of se=
ts:</p>
<ul class=3D"simple">
 <li>
  <p>Conditional: Data objects are automatically grouped based on a logical=
 condition.</p></li>
 <li>
  <p>Manual: Data objects are manually selected to be grouped into a set.&n=
bsp;</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Conditional catalog sets should be used for <u>auto-classification of co=
lumns</u>. The columns should be grouped together based on RegEx patterns. =
When columns are included into a catalog set as members, you can assign cla=
ssifications to the columns in bulk using this set. Any changes to the cata=
log set are propagated to all set members. When new columns get added to Al=
ation, any new column which satisfies a catalog set's condition gets added =
to that set and auto-classified as per the classifications listed in the se=
t.</p>
<p><strong><span style=3D"color:#333333;">Data Sources</span></strong></p>
<p>In Alation, a Data Source represents a configured connection to an under=
lying data system=E2=80=94such as a database, schema, or data warehouse=E2=
=80=94that Alation can scan, profile, and catalog. Each data source defines=
 the metadata connection parameters, authentication method, and scope of me=
tadata extraction (e.g.&nbsp;which schemas, tables, or columns are included=
). Through these data sources, Alation is able to collect and maintain up-t=
o-date metadata about data structures, lineage, and classifications within =
connected platforms such as Snowflake.</p>
<p>Within context of this solution, to enable scanning of the Snowflake BDH=
 platform, <u>separate Alation Data Sources</u> have been configured to ens=
ure <u>logical segregation</u> between Snowflake <u>Pre-Production</u> and =
<u>Snowflake Production</u>&nbsp;metadata scanning.</p>
<ul>
 <li><u>Alation PRE-PROD Data Sources:</u><strong> </strong>The Pre-Product=
ion Alation Data Sources are used to scan and catalog&nbsp;metadata for Sno=
wflake DEV within the Snowflake PRE-PROD account.</li>
 <li><u>Alation PROD Data Sources: </u>The Production Alation Data Sources =
are are used to scan and catalog&nbsp;metadata for Snowflake PPTE and Snowf=
lake PROD (both within the Snowflake PROD account).&nbsp;<br><span style=3D=
"color:#333333;"><br></span></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.1.2PromotionofAlationObject=
sToHigherLogicalEnvironments">8.1.2 Promotion of Alation Objects To Higher =
Logical Environments</h3>
<p><span>Alation operates as a <u>single-instance platform </u>but supports=
 logical separation in terms of scanning <u>Pre-Production</u> and <u>Produ=
ction Environments,</u> in context of this solution. Within this model, <u>=
Catalog Sets</u> and <u>Data Sources</u> represent the logical configuratio=
n used to scan and classify metadata across different environments. These c=
onfigurations allow governance and classification rules to be tested and va=
lidated prior to production rollout.</span></p>
<p><strong>Non-Production Configurations</strong></p>
<p>In the Non-Production context, Alation Catalog Sets and Data Sources wil=
l be configured to scan:</p>
<ul>
 <li>Snowflake PRE-PROD - considered a Non-Production source system.</li>
 <li>Other Non-Production source systems of record =E2=80=94 such as develo=
pment or test databases external to Snowflake.</li>
</ul>
<p>To ensure clarity and maintain control, a naming convention will be intr=
oduced to clearly denote that these Catalog Sets and Data Sources relate to=
 Non-Production systems (e.g., prefixing with <em>NONPROD</em>).</p>
<p><strong>Production Configurations</strong></p>
<p>When transitioning to Production, the same Catalog Sets and Data Sources=
 will need to be copied and reconfigured to support scanning of Production =
source systems, including Snowflake PPTE and Snowflake PROD.</p>
<ul>
 <li>The Catalog Sets will be reused to ensure consistent classification lo=
gic and tagging rules.</li>
 <li>The Data Source objects will be replicated to establish equivalent pro=
duction-level scans against live systems.</li>
</ul>
<p>This ensures alignment between Snowflake PRE-PROD and Snowflake PROD env=
ironments while maintaining environment-specific isolation for metadata sca=
nning and governance.</p>
<p><strong>Promotion Process</strong></p>
<p>The&nbsp;<u>process of promoting changes</u> from the logical Alation NO=
N-PROD configuration in Alation to the logical PROD configuration will requ=
ire a&nbsp;<u>controlled promotion procedure</u> to manage versioning, appr=
oval, and traceability. This process should ensure that changes to Catalog =
Sets, Data Sources, and associated classification logic are reviewed and ap=
proved before being applied to production scanning.&nbsp;While this governa=
nce process is <u>outside the scope</u><strong> </strong>of this solution d=
esign, it is documented as this will be required to enable classification o=
f data in higher environments. This will need to be formally defined and im=
plemented as part of the&nbsp;broader approach for scanning higher environm=
ents within Alation.<br><br></p>
<div role=3D"region" aria-label=3D"Info" class=3D"confluence-information-ma=
cro  confluence-information-macro-information">
 <p class=3D"title conf-macro-render">Key References</p><span role=3D"prese=
ntation" class=3D"aui-icon aui-icon-small aui-iconfont-info-filled confluen=
ce-information-macro-icon"></span>
 <div class=3D"confluence-information-macro-body">
  <p>For further information on the Alation Setup and how classifications w=
ill be determined, refer to <a href=3D"https://confluence.nz.thenational.co=
m/display/BAI/DRC-13+Data+and+information+classification" rel=3D"nofollow">=
Data Risk Control 13</a>.&nbsp; For detailed information on the autoclassif=
ication rules refer to <a href=3D"https://confluence.nz.thenational.com/dis=
play/BAI/%5BADDAMG-15%5D+-+Alation+Catalog+Sets+for+Autoclassification" rel=
=3D"nofollow">Alation Catalog Sets for Autoclassification</a>.&nbsp;</p>
 </div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-scanned_enviornments8.1.3Useo=
fAlationtoScanSnowflakeDEV,PPTE,PROD"><span class=3D"confluence-anchor-link=
" id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-scanned_enviornments"></span>8.=
1.3 Use of Alation to Scan Snowflake DEV, PPTE, PROD</h3>
<p><span>Using a combination of <u>Alation Data Sources </u>and <u>Catalog =
Sets</u>, the proposed solution enables systematic scanning and classificat=
ion of metadata across key Snowflake environments. This approach ensures th=
at data governance and classification processes are embedded throughout the=
 data lifecycle from development through to production, providing consisten=
t visibility and control over all data assets managed within the Snowflake =
BDH platform.</span></p>
<p><span>For completeness, in terms of the different databases and layers t=
hat will be scanned by Alation across different Snowflake Environments the =
following matrix is provided. </span></p>
<p><span>It is important to note that Snowflake SYST will not be scanned gi=
ven the same classifications that are derived from scanning Snowflake DEV w=
ill be applied Snowflake SYST to inform masking rules in both these environ=
ments. This same pattern applies to higher environments where classificatio=
ns that are derived from scanning Snowflake PPTE will be applied Snowflake =
PROD to inform masking rules in both these environments. Snowflake PROD wil=
l only be scanned for compliance purposes, but the result of the scan will =
not directly be used to inform masking rules within this environment. This =
is done to reduce the maintenance overhead and costs associated with scanni=
ng multiple environments through Alation.</span></p>
