ake DEV, Snowflake SYST and Snowflake PROD. [ACCOUNT] denotes the various S=
nowflake Accounts. These are: Snowflake PRE-PROD and Snowflake PROD.</sup><=
/p>
<h2 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.2IntegrationBetweenAlationa=
ndAlationAnalytics(Components2,10)">8.2 Integration Between Alation and Ala=
tion Analytics (Components 2, 10)</h2>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.2.1UseOfAlationAnalytics">8=
.2.1 Use Of Alation Analytics</h3>
<p>Alation Analytics provides a Snowflake-based analytical layer over Alati=
on=E2=80=99s metadata catalog. It contains detailed metadata about scanned =
data sources, including table and column information, data lineage, usage m=
etrics, and classification tags. Within the context of the Snowflake BDH pl=
atform, this metadata=E2=80=94particularly the classification attributes=E2=
=80=94will serve as the authoritative source to determine how data in Snowf=
lake is classified and subsequently governed.</p>
<p>These classifications directly influence data masking policies and role-=
based access controls (RBAC), determining which users or roles are permitte=
d to view sensitive data. By synchronizing Alation=E2=80=99s metadata with =
Snowflake, the platform ensures that classification logic applied during ca=
taloging is consistently enforced within the data platform itself, achievin=
g alignment between data governance intent and technical enforcement.</p>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.2.2UseOfAlationAnalyticsast=
hePreferredIntegrationPattern">8.2.2 Use Of Alation Analytics as the Prefer=
red Integration Pattern&nbsp;</h3>
<p>The use of Alation Analytics is the preferred integration pattern for sy=
nchronizing classification metadata with Snowflake BDH. This approach lever=
ages Snowflake=E2=80=99s native Secure Data Sharing capability to establish=
 a secure and efficient connection between Alation Analytics (source) and S=
nowflake BDH (target).</p>
<p>Because both systems are Snowflake-based, this integration provides a pe=
rformant, secure, and operationally lightweight mechanism for data exchange=
. The secure data share model eliminates the need for complex API managemen=
t, external connectors, or intermediary data movements, thereby aligning wi=
th Snowflake=E2=80=99s zero-copy sharing principle and ensuring data integr=
ity.</p>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.2.3FrequencyandSchedulingof=
UpdatesBetweenAlationandAlationAnalytics">8.2.3 Frequency and Scheduling of=
 Updates Between Alation and Alation Analytics</h3>
<p>Alation Analytics is populated via Alation=E2=80=99s internal ETL proces=
ses, which refresh the metadata warehouse approximately once every <u>24 ho=
urs by default</u>. This cadence ensures that newly classified data, modifi=
ed metadata, or updated catalog entries are consistently reflected in the A=
lation Analytics schema.</p>
<p>However, the ETL <u>schedule is configurable</u> and can be executed mor=
e frequently if operational needs dictate=E2=80=94for example, during major=
 data onboarding activities or classification exercises. In addition, a <u>=
manual ETL refresh</u> can be initiated by Alation administrators in scenar=
ios where immediate synchronization is required, such as after large-scale =
classification updates or catalog set changes.</p>
<p>This flexibility ensures that Snowflake BDH always has access to the lat=
est classification metadata, which can then be automatically applied.</p>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.2.4AlternativeIntegrationPa=
tternsConsidered">8.2.4 Alternative Integration Patterns Considered</h3>
<p>Several alternative integration patterns were evaluated prior to confirm=
ing Alation Analytics as the preferred approach:</p>
<ul>
 <li><u>Alation APIs</u>: The use of Alation=E2=80=99s REST APIs to pull cl=
assification metadata directly into Snowflake was considered. However, this=
 approach was deemed unsuitable for large-scale or frequent synchronization=
, as it relies on API pagination and sequential retrieval processes. From a=
 scalability and performance perspective, this method would not efficiently=
 support enterprise data volumes or near real-time updates.</li>
 <li><u>Direct Integration between Alation and Snowflake</u>: Another optio=
n considered was establishing a direct connector between Alation and Snowfl=
ake to push classification updates. This approach was not fit for purpose, =
as Alation=E2=80=99s direct integration methods are primarily designed for =
field-level updates. The intended use case within BDH requires bulk classif=
ication propagation=E2=80=94for example, updating thousands of columns usin=
g Alation=E2=80=99s Catalog Sets=E2=80=94which the direct integration canno=
t effectively support.</li>
</ul>
<p>Based on these assessments, the Alation Analytics Secure Data Share appr=
oach was selected as the strategic integration model, offering both scalabi=
lity and operational simplicity while aligning with Snowflake=E2=80=99s gov=
ernance and data-sharing best practices.</p>
<h2 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.3IntegrationBetweenAlationA=
nalyticsandSnowflakeBDHviaSecureDataShare(Components3,11)">8.3 Integration =
Between Alation Analytics and Snowflake BDH via Secure Data Share (Componen=
ts 3, 11)</h2>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.3.1SecureDataSharing">8.3.1=
 Secure Data Sharing</h3>
<p>To enable integration of&nbsp;data classification metadata between Alati=
on Analytics and Snowflake BDH, a secure data share will be established fro=
m Alation Analytics into <u>both</u><strong> </strong>the Snowflake PRE-PRO=
D and Snowflake PROD accounts. This secure data share will serve as the pri=
mary mechanism for synchronizing the metadata required to drive classificat=
ion logic, governance reporting, and automated masking policies across the =
Snowflake environments.&nbsp;Given that this data share represents an integ=
ration with a third-party platform (Alation), formal security approval will=
 be required prior to implementation. This approval will ensure that all in=
formation exchange mechanisms, data handling practices, and access controls=
 comply with enterprise security and data governance policies.</p>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.3.2TechnicalArchitectureand=
Prerequisites">8.3.2 Technical Architecture and Prerequisites</h3>
<p>To qualify for the establishment of the secure data share, Alation Analy=
tics and Snowflake BDH must both reside within the <u>same AWS Region</u>. =
This ensures that cross-region data egress does not occur and that Snowflak=
e=E2=80=99s secure data sharing framework can operate natively without addi=
tional replication or networking overhead.</p>
<p><span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=3D"8f42f2ce-9d6e-40fe-92e=
4-249f4d2335af">Both Alation Analytics and the Snowflake BDH accounts are h=
osted within the </span><u><span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=
=3D"8f42f2ce-9d6e-40fe-92e4-249f4d2335af">AWS Sydney Region (ap-southeast-2=
)</span></u><span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=3D"8f42f2ce-9d6e=
-40fe-92e4-249f4d2335af">, satisfying this prerequisite. This regional alig=
nment enables a performant and compliant data-sharing setup, with metadata =
accessible to the Governance Database within Snowflake BDH for downstream p=
rocessing and classification alignment.</span></p>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.3.3DataScopeandSecurityCons=
iderations">8.3.3 Data Scope and Security Considerations</h3>
<p>It is critical to note that the Alation Analytics data share will contai=
n<strong> </strong><u>only metadata</u>, not actual data values. The data s=
hared will include metadata elements such as table names, column names, cla=
ssification codes, and lineage relationships=E2=80=94consistent with Alatio=
n=E2=80=99s purpose as a metadata management and data intelligence platform=
.</p>
<p>Since <u>no actual field level data values</u>, PII (Personally Identifi=
able Information), or PCI (Payment Card Information) will be included in th=
e data share, the <u>data exposure risk is minimal</u>. However, as part of=
 the organisation=E2=80=99s broader data security posture, it has been note=
d that <u>DataShield</u> was previously being explored to scan shared objec=
ts for potential sensitive data.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>
<p>The current request is to establish a <u><em>pattern</em></u> for consum=
ing inbound Snowflake shares where some basic string validation is performe=
d on appropriate fields as new rows are shared. This is done to consistentl=
y sanity check on new rows shared to us via third parties. These include:</=
p>
<ol>
 <li>Alert/notify if new columns added to shared database&nbsp;</li>
 <li>Perform basic validation of appropriate fields (e.g. alert/notify if a=
 string contains an unusual/unexpected character or string such as ; &amp; =
| $ `or strings such as 'powershell.exe', 'cmd.exe', '/bin/sh', 'wget', 'cu=
rl', '../')</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: It is recommended that the design for t=
his is included as part of the next iteration of the design, which will be =
required to enable additional enhancements, refer to: <a href=3D"#BDHClassi=
fication&amp;MaskingDesign-open_items">Section&nbsp;4.1 Open Items For Next=
 Design Iteration</a>.</p>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.3.4AlationSupportAgreements=
andSLAS">8.3.4 Alation Support Agreements and SLAS</h3>
<p>To ensure clarity and transparency, this section outlines how support co=
verage and responsibilities apply to Alation Analytics and its associated d=
ata services.</p>
<ul>
 <li>All Alation managed capabilities, including Alation Analytics and the =
underlying platform services, are governed by the existing BNZ=E2=80=93Alat=
ion Support Agreement. This agreement is the single source of truth for all=
 service coverage, escalation paths, and response expectations.</li>
 <li>If an incident occurs impacting Alation Analytics data availability or=
 integrity (for example, if the data population job fails or data becomes o=
ut of sync), the Alation Support Team will coordinate with BNZ to investiga=
te and resolve the issue in alignment with agreed SLAs.</li>
 <li>At present, there are no separate SLAs specific to the Alation Analyti=
cs Data Share. However, where the data supports core business or regulatory=
 reporting, BNZ may raise incidents as P1 (Critical) or P2 (High) severity =
based on business impact.</li>
 <li>Snowflake related incidents (such as outages, degraded performance, or=
 infrastructure issues impacting the Data Share) are managed directly by Sn=
owflake under their respective support and service agreements.</li>
 <li>In all cases, Alation and BNZ Support Teams will work collaboratively =
to maintain data reliability, restore service continuity, and communicate s=
tatus updates in accordance with defined processes.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.3.5AlationChangeManagementP=
rocess">8.3.5 Alation Change Management Process</h3>
<p>This section is provided to give BNZ transparency and confidence regardi=
ng how changes to the Alation Analytics structure are managed, tracked, and=
 communicated. While these changes are infrequent and typically non-disrupt=
ive, they are documented here for awareness and impact consideration.</p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 10=
2.842%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 12.7687%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 87.2213%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Change Type</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Considerations</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">BNZ Initiated Changes</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>BNZ may introduce Custom Fields or make modifications to Object T=
emplates (e.g., Table, Column, or Dashboard templates) within the Alation p=
latform.</li>
      <li>These customizations automatically extend into Alation Analytics,=
 resulting in new columns being added to corresponding tables in the data s=
hare.=20
       <ul>
        <li>Example: Adding a =E2=80=9CCriticality=E2=80=9D custom field (v=
alues such as Yes/No/Under Review) to Table or Column templates will create=
 a new column in the&nbsp;<code>rdbms_tables</code> table within Alation An=
alytics.</li>
       </ul></li>
      <li>These schema extensions are tenant-specific and driven entirely b=
y BNZ=E2=80=99s configuration of the Alation platform.</li>
      <li>As they are initiated and controlled by BNZ, they are not include=
d in Alation=E2=80=99s global Release Notes, but are fully traceable throug=
h Alation=E2=80=99s metadata model.</li>
      <li>This type of change represents the most frequent and expected sch=
ema evolution for BNZ=E2=80=99s Alation Analytics environment.</li>
     </ul></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Alation Adds New Objects</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>As part of Alation=E2=80=99s ongoing product development, new tab=
les, columns, or data objects may be introduced to support enhanced functio=
nality or analytics coverage (e.g., Data Quality metrics, Document usage, o=
r Governance reporting).</li>
      <li>Alation=E2=80=99s standard product release cadence is monthly, wi=
th a transition planned to a fortnightly schedule to accelerate innovation.=
</li>
      <li>These changes are generally additive and backward-compatible, min=
imizing disruption to existing queries or integrations.</li>
      <li>All such additions are documented in official Alation Release Not=
es, which BNZ can review to assess new opportunities for reporting and anal=
ytics enablement.</li>
     </ul></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Alation Modifies Existing Objects</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>On rare occasions, Alation may update existing tables or columns =
to address bug fixes, performance improvements, or evolving product require=
ments.</li>
      <li>While such modifications are uncommon, they may require BNZ to va=
lidate or adjust downstream reporting, data pipelines, or ETL logic depende=
nt on affected tables.</li>
      <li>Any material structural changes will be communicated via Release =
Notes and Alation Analytics documentation, ensuring BNZ is informed with su=
fficient notice for testing and remediation.</li>
      <li>Historically, these types of changes have been minimal and infreq=
uent, reflecting the maturity and stability of Alation Analytics across our=
 customer base.</li>
     </ul></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h2 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.4UseOfGovernanceDatabasetoS=
toreClassifications(Components4,12)">8.4 Use Of Governance Database to Stor=
e Classifications (Components 4, 12)</h2>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.4.1UseOfGovernanceDatabaseA=
crossSnowflakeEnvironments">8.4.1 Use Of Governance Database Across Snowfla=
ke Environments</h3>
<p><strong>Governance Database To Operate At Account Level</strong></p>
<p>The Governance Database will operate at a&nbsp;<u>Account Level</u> with=
in each Snowflake account to centralize and streamline the application of d=
ata governance policies across environments. A dedicated Governance Databas=
e will exist in both the Snowflake Pre-Production (PRE-PROD) and Snowflake =
Production (PROD) accounts.</p>
<p>Within the Snowflake PRE-PROD account, the classifications determined th=
rough the scanning of the Snowflake DEV environment will be applied to the =
Snowflake SYST environment within the Snowflake PRE-PROD Account Governance=
 Database. Similarly, within the Snowflake PROD account, classifications de=
termined from the Snowflake PPTE environment will be applied to the Snowfla=
ke PROD environment.</p>
<p>This design ensures that data governance and classification are integrat=
ed early in the data product development lifecycle rather than deferred unt=
il production deployment. By elevating the Governance Database to the Accou=
nt Level, the approach also eliminates the need to redeploy governance arte=
facts when promoting code between environments (e.g., from DEV to SYST or f=
rom PPTE to PROD). This promotes consistency, standardisation, and maintain=
ability across the platform. This has also been designed to align with the =
logical environment setup within Alation; where a decision has been made to=
 maintain only PRE-PROD and PROD Catalog sets, rather than maintaining indi=
vidual catalog sets for every physical Snowflake Environment (DEV, SYST, PP=
TE and PROD).&nbsp;</p>
<p>It is important to note that this represents a strategic deviation from =
the existing interim design, where a separate Governance Database exists wi=
thin each individual Snowflake environment (DEV, SYST, PPTE, and PROD). Thi=
s approach simplifies lifecycle management and ensures that classification =
logic and metadata remain consistent and auditable across the environments =
within a given account.&nbsp;To help illustrate how the Governance Database=
 relates to the environments that Alation will scan, the following table ca=
n be referenced:</p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 92=
.2838%;">
