 </table>
</div>
<p><strong>*&nbsp;</strong>Although Snowflake PROD will be scanned by Alati=
on, the result of scan will not be used to determine the actual Classificat=
ions and subsequent Masking within this environment; these will be determin=
ed as part of scanning of Snowflake PPTE. Scanning of Snowflake PROD is con=
ducted to satisfy compliance requirements.&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>&nbsp;</strong></p>
<p><strong>Retention of Existing Objects Within Governance Database</strong=
></p>
<p>Existing objects currently residing within the Governance Database will =
be <u>retained</u> as part of this strategic BDH uplift (e.g. objects in th=
e following schemas: <em>GOV_CLASSIFICATION, GOV_LOGGING, GOV_MASKING, GOV_=
TAGGING</em>); use of these objects are documented here: <a href=3D"https:/=
/confluence.nz.thenational.com/display/BAI/Snowflake+Interim+Tag+Based+Mask=
ing+Policy+Code" rel=3D"nofollow">Snowflake Interim Tag Based Masking Polic=
y Code - Digital, Data &amp; Analytics - BNZ Confluence</a>.&nbsp;The&nbsp;=
<u>Primary Enhancement</u> introduced in the strategic BDH design relates t=
o the process for updating the <em>GOV_CLASSIFICATIONS.CLASSIFICATIONS_DATA=
</em> table, which will now be refreshed automatically using classification=
s sourced from the <u>Alation Analytics Data Share</u> instead of using man=
ual classifications fed through external artifacts. This refresh process an=
d its associated logic are detailed in the subsequent section, which descri=
bes how classifications are extracted, transformed and de-duplicated and th=
en applied to ensure the most current governance metadata is always availab=
le within the platform. Other minor enhancements will be made to the Maskin=
g Rules, refer to: <a href=3D"#BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-masking_=
rul">Section 8.5.1 Key Classifications</a>.&nbsp;</p>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.4.2IntegrationofAlationAnal=
yticsIntotheClassificationTable">8.4.2 Integration of Alation Analytics Int=
o the Classification Table</h3>
<p>This section outlines the baseline SQL pattern used to interpret Alation=
 Analytics tables and propagate the latest data classifications into the pl=
atform=E2=80=99s governance layer. A <u>daily dbt job</u> will be scheduled=
 to read classification records from the Alation Analytics data share, sele=
ct the most recent values per object/column, and apply a <u>de-duplication<=
/u> routine where duplicates exist (e.g., multiple records for the same obj=
ect with overlapping timestamps).</p>
<p>Following extraction and de-duplication, the job will upsert the resolve=
d classifications into <em>GOV_CLASSIFICATIONS.CLASSIFICATIONS_DATA</em> wi=
thin the Governance database. As part of the upsert, '<em>LAST_UPDATE_DATE'=
</em> will be set to the current processing timestamp to provide a reliable=
 watermark for downstream processes. These refreshed classifications enable=
 the masking and access behaviours described in <a href=3D"#BDHClassificati=
on&amp;MaskingDesign-application_of_classification">Section 7.5.3 =E2=80=94=
 Application of Classification Data using dbt Post Hooks and Macros</a>.</p=
>
<div role=3D"region" aria-label=3D"Info" class=3D"confluence-information-ma=
cro  confluence-information-macro-information">
 <p class=3D"title conf-macro-render">Indicative SQL Logic</p><span role=3D=
"presentation" class=3D"aui-icon aui-icon-small aui-iconfont-info-filled co=
nfluence-information-macro-icon"></span>
 <div class=3D"confluence-information-macro-body">
  <p>The SQL included here serves as a&nbsp;<strong>design baseline</strong=
> to communicate intent, integration points, and control logic. The exact S=
QL (object names, join conditions, and windowing logic) will be finalized a=
nd optimized during the <strong>as-built implementation</strong>, informed =
by live Alation Analytics schemas and production data behaviors.</p>
 </div>
</div>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
 <div class=3D"codeHeader panelHeader pdl hide-border-bottom" style=3D"bord=
er-bottom-width: 1px;">
  <b class=3D"code-title">Indicative SQL to Interpret Alation Analytics Tab=
les</b><span class=3D"collapse-source expand-control" style=3D"display:none=
;"><span class=3D"expand-control-icon icon">&nbsp;</span><span class=3D"exp=
and-control-text">Expand source</span></span><span class=3D"collapse-spinne=
r-wrapper"></span>
 </div>
 <div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl hide-toolbar">
  <pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"bru=
sh: sql; gutter: false; collapse: true">select=20
am.object_id,
am.object_type,
am.catalog_set_ids,
cm.catalog_set_property_id as CM_SET_ID,
cm.catalog_set_title,
cm.members_object_type AS object_type,
rc.column_id,
rc.name as column_name,
rc.title as column_title,
rc.table_id,
rt.name as table_name,
rc.schema_id,
rs.name as schema_name,
rc.is_primary_key,
cm.security_classification,
cm.privacy_classification,
cm.tags
from=20
    (
        SELECT
            object_uuid,
            object_id,
            object_type_id,
            object_type,
            f.value::string AS catalog_set_ids
        FROM
            public.alation_set_member,
            LATERAL FLATTEN(input =3D&gt; catalog_set_ids) f
        WHERE
            object_type in ('attribute')
    ) am
JOIN public.catalog_set_membership cm ON am.catalog_set_ids =3D cm.catalog_=
set_property_id=20
JOIN public.rdbms_columns rc ON am.object_id =3D rc.column_id
JOIN public.rdbms_tables rt ON rc.table_id =3D rt.table_id
JOIN public.rdbms_schemas rs ON rt.schema_id =3D rs.schema_id
where
cm.deleted =3D False
order by rc.table_id, rc.schema_id;</pre>
 </div>
</div>
<p><strong>Additional Callouts:</strong></p>
<ul>
 <li><code>CATALOG_SET_MEMBERSHIP</code> table: Values set by the catalog s=
et for all objects will be visible in this table.</li>
 <li><code>RDMBS_SCHEMAS</code> and <code>RDBMS_COLUMNS</code>: Values set =
by custom fields will appear as additional columns in these tables. Additio=
nal other metadata (e.g. whether the field is a Primary Key) will be availa=
ble in this table.</li>
 <li><code>TS_UPDATED</code> column: Multiple values may be returned for a =
filed classification. To deduplicate entries ROW_NUMBER() and TS_UPDATE col=
umn will need to be used to identify the latest classification.</li>
 <li><code>DS_ID</code> column is used to determine which logical environme=
nt the classifications have been applied to; these are either PRE-PROD or P=
ROD</li>
</ul>
<p>The following documentation provided by Alation (<a href=3D"/download/at=
tachments/1968704276/Alation%20Analytics%20V2%20Runbook.pdf?version=3D1&amp=
;modificationDate=3D1760324122819&amp;api=3Dv2" data-linked-resource-id=3D"=
1990690973" data-linked-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D=
"attachment" data-linked-resource-default-alias=3D"Alation Analytics V2 Run=
book.pdf" data-nice-type=3D"PDF Document" data-linked-resource-content-type=
=3D"application/pdf" data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"1968704276" data-=
linked-resource-container-version=3D"82">Alation Analytics V2 Runbook.pdf</=
a>) will be referenced to determine the exact SQL logic to identify how ext=
ract classifications correctly.</p>
<h2 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.5ApplicationofClassificatio=
ns(Components5,13)">8.5 Application of Classifications (Components 5, 13)</=
h2>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-masking_rules8.5.1KeyClassifi=
cationsThatWillInformDataMasking"><span class=3D"confluence-anchor-link" id=
=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-masking_rules"></span>8.5.1 Key Cla=
ssifications That Will Inform Data Masking</h3>
<p>The following table indicates the list of available tags that will be ma=
de available within the Governance Database.</p>
<p>Only the <em>security_classification_code</em> tag will be used to to in=
form whether data needs to be masked or not; note this will be used in conj=
unction with the <span>business_key_flag, primary_key_flag, foreign_key_fla=
g&nbsp; to determine if hashing is required</span>.&nbsp;The actual applica=
tion of this tag to related columns or other objects will be discussed in <=
a href=3D"#BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-application_of_classificatio=
n">Section 8.5.3 Application of Classifications Using DBT Post Hooks &amp; =
Macros</a> and <a href=3D"#BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-RBAC">Sectio=
n 8.6 Role Based Access and Masking of Data.</a> <br><br></p>
<div role=3D"region" aria-label=3D"Info" class=3D"confluence-information-ma=
cro  confluence-information-macro-information">
 <p class=3D"title conf-macro-render">Hashing of Keys</p><span role=3D"pres=
entation" class=3D"aui-icon aui-icon-small aui-iconfont-info-filled conflue=
nce-information-macro-icon"></span>
 <div class=3D"confluence-information-macro-body">
  <p>The following rules below will be used to determine when to mask or ha=
sh data. Note: Hashing of keys is required and is described in more detail =
in <a href=3D"#BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-handling_of_keys">Sectio=
n 8.5.4 Handling of Business Primary and Foreign Keys</a>.</p>
 </div>
</div>
<p><br></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 87=
.1886%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 21.7409%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 15.0804%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 23.626%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 39.5856%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Masking Rules</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Snowflake Tag</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Description</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Pseudo Logic</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><strong>Rule 1:</strong> Determines <u>WHEN<=
/u> to mask or hash data:</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>security_classification_code<br><span>business_key_flag<br>
        primary_key_flag<br>
        foreign_key_flag&nbsp;<br></span></p>
     <p><br></p></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>This policy will be used conjunction with the RBAC model (described=
 in subsequent sections) to control access to data based on the classificat=
ion.</p>
     <p><br></p></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Rule 1.1: Identify Keys:=20
       <ul>
        <li><span>A field is considered a Key if any of the following Snowf=
lake Tag Values are set:<br>
           business_key_flag =3D 'Y' OR primary_key_flag =3D 'Y' OR foreign=
_key_flag =3D 'Y'</span></li>
        <li><span>If none of these flags are Y then the field is not a Key<=
br><br></span></li>
       </ul></li>
      <li>Rule 1.2: Determine Security Classification:<br>
       <ul>
        <li><span>Security Classification <span>HAS a value of 'Unclassifie=
d', 'High Confidential', 'Confidential' OR 'Private'&nbsp;</span></span></l=
i>
        <li><span>Note: 'Unclassified' is treated as equivalent to 'Highly =
Confidential' =E2=80=94 both represent the highest sensitivity classificati=
on and are handled identically in all logic.<br><br></span></li>
       </ul></li>
      <li><span>Rule 1.3 Role Based Access:</span>
       <ul>
        <li><span>The user=E2=80=99s role determines which classifications =
can be viewed in clear text.<br>
           If the user does not have permission to view a given classificat=
ion, the data will be either masked (non-key) or hashed (key).<span><br></s=
pan></span>
         <div class=3D"table-wrap">
          <table class=3D"wrapped confluenceTable" data-mce-resize=3D"false=
">
           <colgroup>
            <col>
            <col>
            <col>
           </colgroup>
           <tbody>
            <tr>
             <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Role</th>
             <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Security Classificati=
on</th>
             <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">User Capability</th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
             <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Highly Confidential</td>
             <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Unclassified, Highly Confidential o=
r Lower</td>
             <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Can View Data In Clear</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
             <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Confidential</td>
             <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Confidential or Lower</td>
             <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Can View Data In Clear</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
             <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Private</td>
             <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Private or Lower</td>
             <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Can View Data In Clear</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
             <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Public</td>
             <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Public</td>
             <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Can View Data In Clear</td>
            </tr>
           </tbody>
          </table>
         </div></li>
       </ul></li>
      <li>Rule 1.4: Determine Action:<br>
       <p>Each field will result in one of three outcomes:</p>
       <ol>
        <li>Masking Required =E2=80=93 Applies to non-key fields where the =
user=E2=80=99s role does not have permission to view the classification in =
clear text.</li>
        <li>Hashing Required =E2=80=93 Applies to key fields (Rule 1) where=
 the user=E2=80=99s role does not have permission to view the classificatio=
n in clear text.</li>
        <li>No Masking or Hashing Required =E2=80=93 Applies when the user=
=E2=80=99s role is allowed to view the classification in clear text.<br><br=
></li>
       </ol></li>
      <li>Combined Rules to Form Pseudo Logic:</li>
     </ul>
     <p style=3D"margin-left: 40.0px;">#Rule 1.1: Determine if field is a K=
ey#<br>
       &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<em>&nbsp;isKey =3D (</em><br><em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbs=
p;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;data_field.business_key_flag =3D 'Y' OR</em=
><br><em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;data_field.primary=
_key_flag =3D 'Y' OR</em><br><em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=
&nbsp;data_field.foreign_key_flag =3D 'Y'</em><br><em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nb=
sp;)</em><br><br>
       &nbsp;#Rule 1.2 : Get Security Classification#&nbsp;<br>
       &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<em>classification =3D data_field.security_c=
lassification</em><br><br>
       &nbsp;#Rule 1.3: Get User Role#<br>
       &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<em>userRole =3D CURRENT_USER_ROLE()</em><br=
><em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;canViewClear =3D FALSE</em><br><br><em>&nbsp;&=
nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;IF userRole =3D=3D 'Highly Confidential' AND classificatio=
n in ('Highly Confidential', 'Unclassified')&nbsp; </em><br><em>&nbsp; &nbs=
p; or Lower Classification:&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</em><br><em>&n=
bsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;canViewClear =3D TRUE</em><br=
><em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;ELSE IF userRole =3D=3D 'Confidential' AND cla=
ssification =3D 'Confidential' or Lower Classification:</em><br><em>&nbsp;&=
nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;canViewClear =3D TRUE</em><br><em>=
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;ELSE IF userRole =3D=3D 'Private' AND classificatio=
n =3D 'Private' or Lower Classification:</em><br><em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbs=
p;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;canViewClear =3D TRUE</em><br>
       &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;ELSE&nbsp;<br>
       &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;canViewClear =3D FAL=
SE<br><br>
       &nbsp;#Rule 1.4: Determine Action#<br>
       &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<em>&nbsp;IF canViewClear:</em><br><em>&nbsp;&nbsp=
;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;action =3D 'No Masking or Hashing Requ=
ired'</em><br><em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;ELSE:</em><br><em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nb=
sp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;IF isKey:</em><br><em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&n=
bsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;action =3D 'Hashing Req=
uired'</em><br><em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;ELSE:</e=
m><br><em>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp=
;&nbsp;action =3D 'Masking Required'</em></p></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><strong>Rule 2:</strong> Determines <u>HOW</=
u> to mask data per data type:</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">anchor_tag</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">This policy will mask data for different dat=
atypes (String, Number, Data) will be assigned to a single anchor_tag that =
is applied to all columns. &nbsp;</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>If Masking Required<br>
       Then</p>
     <p>Case When Datatype =3D 'String' Then '**MASKED**'<br>
       Case When If Datatype =3D 'Number' then '-1'<br>
       Case When Datatype =3D 'Date' then '0001-01-01'</p>
     <p>Else Masking Not Required</p></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<p><u><span style=3D"color:#333333;">Note: </span></u></p>
<ul>
 <li><span style=3D"color:#333333;">All other tags will not be used to info=
rm masking. Instead the related classifications in Alation are used for GIR=
P compliance only.</span></li>
 <li><span style=3D"color:#333333;">The&nbsp;<code>Unclassified</code> tag =
value is a unique tag value that has been introduced within the governance =
database within Snowflake only; this classification does not reside within =
Alation. This tag value will be applied to all objects by default to defens=
ively ensure that data that that is unclassified remains masked by default =
until the data is classified. This is explained in <a href=3D"#BDHClassific=
ation&amp;MaskingDesign-default_classification">Section&nbsp;8.5.2 Default =
Classification For Unclassified Data &amp; Use of Anchor Tag For Masking of=
 Data</a>.&nbsp;</span></li>
</ul>
<p><span style=3D"color:#333333;">For completeness the following table is p=
rovide to document this available tags and list of allowable values.</span>=
</p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 63=
.5659%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 40.3979%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 42.5241%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 17.0844%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Snowflake Tag</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Allowable Values</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Used To Inform Over Masking So=
lution</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>security_classification_code</code></t=
d>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>Private, Public, Confidential, Highly =
Confidential, Unclassified</code></td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#abf5d1 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
abf5d1" title=3D"Background color : Light green 100%">
     <p title=3D""><strong>Yes</strong></p></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>anchor_tag</code></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>None</code></td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#abf5d1 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
abf5d1" title=3D"Background color : Light green 100%"><strong>Yes</strong><=
/td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>zone_tag</code></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>Examples: PTP, Loans, CustEng</code></=
td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#abf5d1 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
abf5d1" title=3D"Background color : Light green 100%"><strong title=3D"">Ye=
s</strong></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>business_key_flag</code></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>Y,Empty</code></td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#abf5d1 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
abf5d1" title=3D"Background colour : Light green 100%"><strong title=3D"">Y=
es</strong></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>primary_key_flag</code></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>Y,Empty</code></td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#abf5d1 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
abf5d1" title=3D"Background colour : Light green 100%"><strong title=3D"">Y=
es</strong></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>foreign_key_flag</code></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>Y,Empty</code></td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#abf5d1 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
abf5d1" title=3D"Background colour : Light green 100%"><strong title=3D"">Y=
es</strong></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>privacy_identifier_flag</code></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>Y, N</code></td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#ffebe6 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
ffebe6" title=3D"Background color : Light red 35%"><strong title=3D"">No</s=
trong></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>pci_dss_compliance_identifier_flag</co=
de></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>Y, N</code></td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#ffebe6 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
ffebe6" title=3D"Background color : Light red 35%"><strong title=3D"">No</s=
trong></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>government_identifier_flag</code></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>Y, N</code></td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#ffebe6 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
ffebe6" title=3D"Background color : Light red 35%"><strong title=3D"">No</s=
trong></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code><span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" =
data-ref=3D"32abdea3-060d-4373-8adb-33844d306bc6">sensitive_personal_identi=
fier_flag</span></code></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>Y, N</code></td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#ffebe6 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
ffebe6" title=3D"Background color : Light red 35%"><strong title=3D"">No</s=
trong></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>health_identifier_flag</code></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>Y, N</code></td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#ffebe6 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
ffebe6" title=3D"Background color : Light red 35%"><strong title=3D"">No</s=
trong></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>credit_identifier_flag</code></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>Y, N</code></td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#ffebe6 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
ffebe6" title=3D"Background color : Light red 35%"><strong title=3D"">No</s=
trong></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>credentials_identifer_flag</code></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><code>Y, N</code></td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#ffebe6 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
ffebe6" title=3D"Background color : Light red 35%"><strong title=3D"">No</s=
trong></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-default_classification8.5.2De=
faultClassificationForUnclassifiedData&amp;UseofAnchorTagForMaskingofData">=
<span class=3D"confluence-anchor-link" id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingD=
esign-default_classification"></span>8.5.2 Default Classification For Uncla=
ssified Data &amp; Use of Anchor Tag For Masking of Data</h3>
<p><u>Rule 1</u>: To defensively protect data, any column that has not yet =
been scanned by Alation, the <em>security_classification_code </em>must hav=
e the '<em>Unclassified'</em> tag value recorded against it. This will ensu=
re that only privileged developers using the '<em>Highly Confidential Role<=
/em>' will be able see unclassified data (described in subsequent sections.=
 Effectively, unclassified data will by default be treated as '<em>Highly C=
onfidential</em>' data by default until the data has been classified by Ala=
tion and the matching Tag Values applied.</p>
<p><u>Rule 2</u><strong>:</strong> In addition masking policies that are dr=
iven off an attributes datatype will be enforced using a single <em>anchor_=
tag</em>.</p>
<p>To achieve this, as part of the Zone Automation through Flyway the <em>s=
ecurity_classification_code</em> tag with tag value <em>'Unclassified'&nbsp=
;</em> and the <em>anchor_tag</em> will need to be applied at the database =
level so that all lower objects in the hierarchy (tables and all columns in=
herit this value). To help demonstrate this the following diagram is provid=
ed:</p>
<p><map id=3D"gliffy-map-1983185498-9973" name=3D"gliffy-map-1983185498-997=
3"></map></p>
<table width=3D"100%" class=3D"gliffy-macro-table">
 <tbody>
  <tr>
   <td>
    <table class=3D"gliffy-macro-inner-table">
     <caption align=3D"bottom">
     </caption>
     <tbody>
      <tr>
       <td><img style=3D"border: none; width: 600px;" usemap=3D"#gliffy-map=
-1983185498-9973" src=3D"e31acfbed7d0cc442987eb6f4f0566f54375669812a1ad8656=
6eb584205e3247" alt=3D"" class=3D"gliffy-macro-image" width=3D"468" height=
=3D"219"></td>
      </tr>
     </tbody>
    </table></td>
  </tr>
 </tbody>
</table>
<p></p>
<p><span>If applied at =E2=80=98Database Level=E2=80=99 based on </span><a =
class=3D"external-link" href=3D"https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/ob=
ject-tagging/inheritance" rel=3D"nofollow" title=3D"https://docs.snowflake.=
com/en/user-guide/object-tagging/inheritance"><span>Snowflake Documentation=
 =E2=80=93 Tag Inheritance</span></a><span>:</span></p>
<ul>
 <li><span>A descendant of an object in the hierarchy inherits tags from it=
s ancestors. For example, a schema in an account inherits tags set on the a=
ccount.&nbsp;</span></li>
 <li><span>Therefore if applied at =E2=80=98Database' level, all Schemas, T=
ables, Columns will inherit the Default Classification.&nbsp;</span></li>
 <li><span>It=E2=80=99s possible to override the value of an inherited tag =
on a given object by&nbsp;</span><a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"https:/=
/docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/object-tagging/work.html" rel=3D"nofollow=
" title=3D"https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/object-tagging/work.htm=
l"><span>manually setting</span></a><span> the tag on the object; this will=
 occur once the data has been classified within Alation, made available to =
Snowflake and applied to the related columns. In other words, if a table co=
lumn inherits the<strong> </strong><u>Tag</u> named&nbsp;</span><em>securit=
y_classification_code </em><span>with a tag <u>default tag value</u> of '</=
span><em>Unclassified'&nbsp;</em>the tag can be updated with a more specifi=
c Tag Values such as&nbsp;<em>'Private', 'Public', 'Confidential' or 'Highl=
y Confidential'</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p><u>Note</u>: The default classification logic will only apply to the fol=
lowing layers: <em>LANDING, RAW, FOUNDATION, CURATED</em>. Transform is a p=
rotected database as documented in <a href=3D"#BDHClassification&amp;Maskin=
gDesign-transform_db">Section: 8.6.2 Role Based Specifically For Developer =
Role Within TRANSFORM Database</a>. All other databases are used to store m=
etadata to enable platform operations. This is described in previous <a hre=
f=3D"#BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-scanned_enviornments">Section: 8.=
1.3 Use of Alation to Scan Snowflake DEV, PPTE, PROD</a>.</p>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-application_of_classification=
8.5.3ApplicationofClassificationsUsingDBTPostHooks&amp;Macros"><span class=
=3D"confluence-anchor-link" id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-appli=
cation_of_classification"></span>8.5.3 Application of Classifications Using=
 DBT Post Hooks &amp; Macros</h3>
<p>This section outlines how table- and column-level classifications (tags)=
 are applied to Snowflake objects via dbt. The classification process ensur=
es that metadata changes=E2=80=94particularly security classifications=E2=
=80=94are automatically propagated from the central classification reposito=
ry (Alation-sourced) to Snowflake in a consistent and controlled manner.</p=
>
<p>The design supports two scenarios:</p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 82=
.0106%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 22.0953%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 42.7011%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 23.138%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 12.016%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Scenario</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Description</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Update Mechanism</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Requires Enhancement</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Scenario 1: Daily Scheduled Jobs</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>These are dbt models that run on a daily schedule, where tag update=
s can naturally flow through post-execution hooks.</p></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Schedule Airflow job that calls scheduled dbt job</li>
      <li>dbt Post-Hook Macro after each dbt Run</li>
     </ul></td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#ffebe6 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
ffebe6" title=3D"Background color : Light red 35%">
     <p title=3D""><strong>No</strong></p></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Scenario 2: Non-Daily Scheduled Jobs</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>These are dbt models that do not run daily (e.g.&nbsp;weekly, month=
ly, or ad hoc jobs), where additional logic is required to detect classific=
ation updates and apply them proactively</p></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Unscheduled Airflow job that calls dbt Macro</li>
     </ul></td>
    <td class=3D"highlight-#abf5d1 confluenceTd" data-highlight-colour=3D"#=
abf5d1"><strong>Yes</strong></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h4 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.5.3.1Scenario1:UpdatingClas=
sificationsfordbtJobsThatRunDaily"><strong>8.5.3.1 Scenario 1: Updating Cla=
ssifications for dbt Jobs That Run Daily</strong></h4>
<p>The existing dbt implementation leverages a post-hook macro that runs im=
mediately after each model execution. This macro:</p>
<ul>
 <li>Reads classification information from a central classification table (=
populated from Alation).</li>
 <li>Applies Snowflake tags (e.g., <code>security_classification_code</code=
>) to the relevant tables and columns.</li>
</ul>
<p>The macro consists of two main functional components:</p>
<ol>
 <li>Classification Querying: Retrieves the applicable classification entri=
es based on the executing model=E2=80=99s name, schema, and database.</li>
 <li>Tag Application: Issues Snowflake tag-alteration statements to set or =
update tags at table and column level.</li>
</ol>
<p>This approach ensures that for dbt jobs running daily, any classificatio=
n change is automatically picked up within 24 hours during the next schedul=
ed run.</p>
<p><u>Key Limitation:</u></p>
<ul>
 <li>The current mechanism assumes that dbt models run frequently. For pipe=
lines with longer cadences (e.g., weekly or monthly), classification change=
s are not reflected until the next model execution. This results in delays =
between when a classification is updated and when it becomes effective in S=
nowflake.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.5.3.2Scenario2:UpdatingClas=
sificationsfordbtJobsThatDoNotRunDaily"><strong>8.5.3.2 Scenario 2: Updatin=
g Classifications for dbt Jobs That Do Not Run Daily</strong></h4>
<p><u>Enhancement Objective</u></p>
<p>The enhanced design ensures that any classification updates made in Alat=
ion are reflected in Snowflake <u>without relying on the model execution fr=
equency.</u> It introduces the ability to detect which tables have not rece=
ntly had their metadata (DDL) updated and apply the necessary tags independ=
ently of model runs.</p>
<p><u>Key Design Principles</u></p>
<ol>
 <li>Macro Extension:=20
  <ul>
   <li>The existing&nbsp;<code>apply_tag</code> macro will be enhanced to a=
ccept&nbsp;<u>time-based parameters</u> (e.g., a =E2=80=9Csince date=E2=80=
=9D or =E2=80=9Cmaximum age=E2=80=9D) instead of relying solely on the mode=
l name.</li>
   <li>This allows the process to identify tables whose DDL has not been al=
tered within a defined time window (e.g., seven days).</li>
   <li>It enables classification updates to be applied even when the corres=
ponding dbt job has not recently executed.</li>
  </ul></li>
 <li>Use of Snowflake Information Schema:=20
  <ul>
   <li>The macro will query the <code>INFORMATION_SCHEMA.TABLES</code> view=
 to determine the last modification timestamp (<code>LAST_ALTERED</code>) o=
f each table. Further documentation on usage of this Information Schema tab=
le is located here: <a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"https://docs.snowfla=
ke.com/en/sql-reference/info-schema/tables" rel=3D"nofollow">TABLES view | =
Snowflake Documentation</a></li>
   <li>For daily models, this field is updated frequently.</li>
   <li><span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=3D"9740be83-d9a2-4cfb=
-9ccd-9448699d673c">For infrequently run models, </span><code><span class=
=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=3D"9740be83-d9a2-4cfb-9ccd-9448699d673c=
">LAST_ALTERED</span></code><span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=
=3D"9740be83-d9a2-4cfb-9ccd-9448699d673c"> will indicate that a table has n=
ot been updated for some time, allowing targeted re-tagging. <code>LAST_DDL=
</code> may also be suitable, this will be validated during build.</span></=
li>
  </ul></li>
 <li>Classification Delta Logic:=20
  <ul>
   <li>The process will identify classification records in the central <cod=
e>CLASSIFICATION_DATA</code> table in the Governance Database and will use =
the <code>LAST_UPDATED_DATE</code> to identify classifications entries that=
 have been updated after a specific date (e.g., within the past week or mon=
th).</li>
   <li>These records are joined with the Information Schema results to dete=
rmine which Snowflake objects are impacted by recent classification updates=
.</li>
  </ul></li>
 <li>Selective Tag Application:=20
  <ul>
   <li>Only tables and columns where the classification value differs from =
the currently applied tag will be updated.</li>
   <li>This ensures the process is targeted, avoiding unnecessary DDL chang=
es and overhead in processing and performance.</li>
  </ul></li>
 <li>Controlled Execution via Airflow:<br>
   A new Airflow job will be introduced to invoke the enhanced macro.=20
  <ul>
   <li>Initially, this job will <u>not be scheduled</u> automatically to co=
nserve compute resources; given each time the cluster spins up compute reso=
urces are used; this may be wasteful when nothing has changed.</li>
   <li>It can be triggered on demand by the data governance or engineering =
team whenever significant classification updates occur in Alation; e.g. whe=
n the governance teams confirms critical re-classifications or an incident =
requires immediate re-classification.</li>
   <li>In future iterations, the job can be e set to run periodically (e.g.=
, weekly, monthly) noting that this will also process daily changes; subseq=
uently the processing overhead will need to be accepted.</li>
  </ul></li>
</ol>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-handling_of_keys8.5.4Handling=
ofBusiness,PrimaryandForeignKeys"><span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" dat=
a-ref=3D"1f0e4259-7c6c-46b0-be00-372c5cbfb93a"><span class=3D"confluence-an=
chor-link" id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-handling_of_keys"></sp=
an>8.5.4 Handling of Business, Primary and Foreign Keys</span></h3>
<p>The management of <u>Business Keys (BKs),</u> <u>Primary Keys (PKs)</u> =
and/or <u>Foreign Keys (FKs)</u> within the Snowflake platform requires del=
iberate design to balance data security, classification compliance, and dev=
eloper productivity. An example of this is where: &nbsp;'Customer No' or 'A=
ccount No' are identified as business keys and are required to join across =
tables. If the Business Key is&nbsp; classified as '<em>Confidential</em>' =
or above, a standard Developer Role (Private Role) or Standard Analyst&nbsp=
; Role (Private Role) will see data returned as '**Masked**' (for strings) =
or -1 (for numbers) for every value. &nbsp;This will make effective joining=
 on these keys across datasets unusable.&nbsp;</p>
<p>Consequently, unless a developer holds the appropriate <u>RBAC role</u> =
(e.g., <em>Highly Confidential Role</em> or <em>Confidential Role</em>), th=
e values within those key columns will be <u>Masked</u>. To mitigate this c=
hallenge, two solution options were assessed to determine the most effectiv=
e and scalable approach for managing key-based relationships while maintain=
ing compliance with data classification policies. A decision was made to to=
 hash Business Keys, Primary Keys &amp; Foreign keys to protect the underly=
ing data must still enforcing the integrity of data allowing for cross tabl=
e joins. This is depicted in the below solution diagram, with the options t=
hat were considered in the table below.<br><br></p>
<p><map id=3D"gliffy-map-2040366484-8436" name=3D"gliffy-map-2040366484-843=
6"></map></p>
<table width=3D"100%" class=3D"gliffy-macro-table">
 <tbody>
  <tr>
   <td>
    <table class=3D"gliffy-macro-inner-table">
     <caption align=3D"bottom">
     </caption>
     <tbody>
      <tr>
       <td><img style=3D"border: none; width: 600px;" usemap=3D"#gliffy-map=
-2040366484-8436" src=3D"7b7a2afc117926adaab775046476ebcc7d7b74d72b4f7e0af5=
ef04ef105bec92" alt=3D"" class=3D"gliffy-macro-image" width=3D"468" height=
=3D"211"></td>
      </tr>
     </tbody>
    </table></td>
  </tr>
 </tbody>
</table>
<p></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 79=
.5279%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 21.3593%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 78.6617%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Option&nbsp;</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Description</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>Option 1: Hashing of:&nbsp;</p>
     <ul>
      <li>Business Keys. Primary Keys &amp; Foreign Keys</li>
     </ul>
     <p><strong>(Selected Approach)</strong></p></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>This approach introduces hashing of BKs, PKs and FKs to provide a u=
niform and classification-agnostic way to join tables, where the value is a=
 business key and is classified as <em>'Highly Confidential'</em> or <em>'C=
onfidential'</em>. This is useful for standard developers or standard analy=
st roles. This is described in further detail in the subsequent section.&nb=
sp;</p></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p><span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=3D"a37855c3-f00e-453=
d-bbe2-7f7c922f26aa">Option 2: Role-Based Access to:</span></p>
     <ul>
      <li><span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=3D"a37855c3-f00e-4=
53d-bbe2-7f7c922f26aa">Business Keys, Primary Keys &amp; Foreign Keys&nbsp;=
</span></li>
     </ul></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>This approach extends the existing role-based access control (RBAC)=
 framework to govern access to BKs, PKs and FKs based on their data classif=
ication level. Users that require access to&nbsp;<em>'Highly Confidential'<=
/em> or <em>'Confidential' </em>data will be assigned the appropriate role.=
 This is described in further detail in the subsequent section.&nbsp;</p></=
td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h4 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.5.4.1DetailedOverviewofOpti=
ons"><br>
  8.5.4.1 Detailed Overview of Options</h4>
<p>For completeness, the options are described in further detail below, inc=
luding sub-options where relevant.<br><br></p>
<p><strong>Option 1: Hashing of Keys (Selected Approach)</strong></p>
<p><span>The selected approach introduces hashing for Business Keys, Primar=
y Keys, and Foreign Keys to allow secure, classification-agnostic joins bet=
ween tables.&nbsp;This method enables consistent join operations without ex=
posing sensitive key values to users with lower data access privileges.</sp=
an></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 79=
.6061%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 17.9216%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 18.3084%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 26.3022%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 37.4549%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Classification Of Key</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Conditions</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Displayed Value</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">User Capability</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Highly Confidential</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>User holds <em>Confidential Role or Lo=
wer</em></span></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Hashed value displayed</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>Can perform joins without seeing real =
key</span></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Confidential</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>User holds <em>Private Role or Lower</=
em></span></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>Hashed value displayed</span></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>Can perform joins without seeing real =
key</span></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Private</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>User holds <em>Public Role&nbsp;</em><=
/span></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>Hashed value displayed</span></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>Can perform joins without seeing real =
key</span></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Highly Confidential</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>User holds <em>Highly</em> <em>Confide=
ntial Role</em></span></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Real value displayed</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>Can perform joins with full visibility=
 of real key</span></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Confidential</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>User holds <em>Confidential Role</em><=
/span></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Real value displayed</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>Can perform joins with full visibility=
 of real key</span></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Private</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>User holds <em>Private Role</em></span=
></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Real value displayed</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>Can perform joins with full visibility=
 of real key</span></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Public</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>User holds <em>Public Role</em></span>=
</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Real value displayed</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>Can perform joins with full visibility=
 of real key</span></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<p><strong>Key Characteristics:</strong></p>
<ul>
 <li>Hashing will be applied using a one-way function (e.g. SHA2 or MD5) wi=
thin Snowflake=E2=80=99s Dynamic Data Masking policies.</li>
 <li>Tags such as <code>business_key_flag</code>, <code>primary_key_flag</c=
ode>, and <code>foreign_key_flag</code> will be applied to facilitate polic=
y-driven masking.</li>
 <li>Developers can still join tables on hashed key values, maintaining con=
sistent functionality across roles.</li>
 <li>Ensures joinability across datasets irrespective of data classificatio=
n.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Rationale:</strong></p>
<ul>
 <li>Enables consistent joins across classified datasets without requiring =
privileged access.</li>
 <li>Reduces dependency on role-based visibility of sensitive columns.</li>
 <li>Provides simplified development workflows and stronger data protection=
.</li>
 <li>Enforces classification-driven governance through automated tagging an=
d masking policies.<br><br></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Additional Callouts:</strong></p>
<ul>
 <li>Setting Constraints for Composite Keys via dbt YAML:<strong> </strong>=
It has been observed that composite keys (e.g., multiple primary keys) defi=
ned in dbt YAML files do not materialize as constraints in Snowflake. Given=
 this is a common pattern across our data products, a dbt macro will nee to=
 be developed to enable composite key enforcement.</li>
 <li>Exposure of Foreign Keys via Alation Analytics:<strong style=3D"letter=
-spacing: 0.0px;"> </strong>While the <code style=3D"letter-spacing: 0.0px;=
">rdbms_columns</code><span style=3D"letter-spacing: 0.0px;"> table in Alat=
ion Analytics includes an </span><code style=3D"letter-spacing: 0.0px;">is_=
primary_key</code><span style=3D"letter-spacing: 0.0px;"> flag, the corresp=
onding foreign key metadata is not currently exposed but should be. This sh=
ould be raised as an enhancement request to ensure consistent visibility of=
 both primary and foreign key relationships within Alation Analytics.</span=
></li>
</ul>
<p><strong><br>
   Option 2: Role-Based Access to Keys (Not Selected)</strong></p>
<p><span>This approach extends the existing Role-Based Access Control (RBAC=
) model to govern access to business keys according to their classification=
 level.&nbsp;Access to key values and the ability to perform joins would de=
pend on the user=E2=80=99s assigned role.</span></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 79=
.4522%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 18.4731%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 17.1812%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 26.9991%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 37.3337%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Classification of Key</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Required Role</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Displayed Value</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">User Capability</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Highly Confidential</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Highly Confidential Role</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Real value displayed</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>Can perform joins with full visibility=
 of real key</span></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Confidential</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Confidential Role</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Real value displayed</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>Can perform joins with full visibility=
 of real key</span></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Private</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Private Role</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Real value displayed</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>Can perform joins with full visibility=
 of real key</span></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Public</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Public Role</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Real value displayed</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd"><span>Can perform joins with full visibility=
 of real key</span></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<p><strong>Key Characteristics:</strong></p>
<ul>
 <li>Joins between datasets remain secure, traceable, and compliant with da=
ta classification policies.</li>
 <li>Leverages existing RBAC integrations with Entra ID and Snowflake for p=
rovisioning and auditability.</li>
 <li>Minimises architectural change by extending the current access framewo=
rk.</li>
 <li>The volume of keys in each classification tier is yet to be determined=
 and requires detailed data profiling.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Rationale:</strong></p>
<ul>
 <li>While this approach aligns closely with existing security models and m=
aintains simplicity, it introduces a&nbsp;risk of role inflation of access =
to privileged roles.<br>
   Developers and analysts may require elevated access (e.g. =E2=80=9CConfi=
dential=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9CHighly Confidential=E2=80=9D roles) simply to =
perform joins, which dilutes the principle of least privilege and creates a=
n unbalanced RBAC structure.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.5.4.2Option1-HashingofKeysS=
ub-OptionsForIdentificationandHashingofBusinessKeys,PrimaryKeys&amp;Foreign=
Keys">8.5.4.2 Option 1 - Hashing of Keys Sub-Options For Identification and=
 Hashing of Business Keys, Primary Keys &amp; Foreign Keys</h4>
<p>For completeness, the following section documents the sub-options that w=
ere considered as part of Option 2. This section documents the separate ide=
ntification options that were considered for Business Keys and separately P=
rimary Keys/Foreign Keys.</p>
<p><strong><br>
   Option 1.1: Identification Of Business Keys<br></strong></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 79=
.8112%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 21.5413%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 28.1645%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 24.4992%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 25.7829%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Option</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Description</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Benefit</th>
    <th scope=3D"col" class=3D"confluenceTh">Cons</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Option 1: Identify Business Keys Through Ala=
tion<br><br><strong>(Selected Option)</strong></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>Within Alation:</p>
     <ul>
      <li>Create a new Catalogue Set in Alation to manage identification of=
 Business Keys using a new attribute within this catalogue set <code>busine=
ss_key_flag:Y</code> <code>or empty</code>.</li>
      <li>Define business key classifications (e.g.&nbsp;<code>Customer_No_=
Tag</code>, <code>Account_No_Tag</code>, etc.) and propagate them to Snowfl=
ake via the existing process that has already been defined for other classi=
fications</li>
     </ul>
     <p>Within Snowflake:</p>
     <ul>
      <li>Create a&nbsp;<code>business_key_flag</code> tag ready to be appl=
ied once the classification is provided by Alation.</li>
      <li>Create a set of Masking policies linked directly to the&nbsp;<cod=
e>business_key_flag</code>&nbsp;tag.</li>
     </ul></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Enables dynamic updates by DM&amp;G without redeployment of dbt m=
odels.</li>
      <li>Centralized management through the Data Catalogue.</li>
      <li>Clear alignment with data governance standards.</li>
     </ul></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Requires management by the DM&amp;G team and designed steward, fo=
rtunately this is limited to only a discreet list of keys (e.g. in the magn=
itude of 20-30 business keys).</li>
     </ul></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Option 2: Identify Business Keys Through Sno=
wflake</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <p>Within Snowflake Only:</p>
     <ul>
      <li>Manage identification directly in Snowflake via naming convention=
s (e.g. naming convention added to Snowflake fields such as <code>_BK</code=
>) and attach masking policies accordingly.</li>
      <li>Create a set of Masking policies linked directly to the naming co=
nvention of the field.</li>
     </ul></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Simple implementation leveraging naming standards.</li>
      <li>Supports all key types consistently.</li>
     </ul></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>No synchronization with Alation Data Catalogue.</li>
      <li>Knowledge not shared centrally across data product teams or analy=
st teams, consuming the data.</li>
     </ul></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<p><strong><br>
   Option 1.2: Identification Of Primary Keys &amp; Foreign Keys<br></stron=
g></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 79=
.8625%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 21.4632%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 28.0821%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 24.4192%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 26.0235%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Option</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Description</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Benefit</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Cons</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Option 1: Identify PKs and FKs Through Alati=
on<br><br><strong>(Selected Option)</strong></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Alation scans Snowflake metadata to identify PKs and FKs (defined=
 originally in dbt YAML files but materialized in Snowflake tables).</li>
      <li>Metadata is extracted within Alation by configuring <em>Data Sour=
ce Settings =E2=86=92 Customize Extraction Scope</em>.</li>
      <li>PKs are then published to&nbsp;<code>rdbms_columns</code> within =
Alation Analytics.&nbsp;</li>
      <li>Using the the data available within Alation Analytics the <code>p=
rimary_key_flag:Y</code> and <code>foreign_key_flag:Y </code>tag and tag va=
lues are set within Snowflake.&nbsp;</li>
      <li>The masking policy linked to these tags will then be applied.</li=
>
     </ul></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Consistent, automated identification process aligned with the Bus=
iness Keys identification approach.</li>
      <li>Metadata committed back to Alation, enabling knowledge sharing ac=
ross teams.</li>
      <li>No need for custom Snowflake views.</li>
     </ul></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Note: Requires a minor enhancement to support&nbsp;<code>is_forei=
gn_key</code> flag via Alation Analytics</li>
     </ul></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Option 2: Identify PKs and FKs Through Snowf=
lake</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Apply a consistent naming convention (e.g. <code>_PK</code>, <cod=
e>_FK</code>) and dynamically apply masking policies within Snowflake.</li>
     </ul></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Promotes strong modelling discipline.</li>
      <li>Supports all key types.</li>
      <li>Simplifies technical implementation.</li>
     </ul></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Inconsistent with Business Keys identification approach.</li>
      <li>No metadata sharing with Alation Catalogue and by extension knowl=
edge sharing with wider teams who may use the same tables.</li>
     </ul></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Option 3: Identify PKs and FKs Through dbt Y=
AML Files</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Extract constraints directly from dbt YAML files.</li>
     </ul></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Avoids need for naming conventions.</li>
      <li>Similar technical benefits to Option 2.</li>
     </ul></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Inconsistent with Business Keys identification approach.</li>
      <li>Alation cannot integrate directly with YAML files</li>
     </ul>
     <ul>
      <li>No metadata sharing with Alation Catalogue and by extension knowl=
edge sharing with wider teams who may use the same tables.</li>
     </ul></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Option 4: Manual Identification by DM&amp;G<=
/td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Primary and Foreign Keys manually identified and managed by the D=
M&amp;G team.</li>
     </ul></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>None of significance.</li>
     </ul></td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ul>
      <li>Highly manual and time-consuming.</li>
      <li>Introduces operational overhead and potential inconsistency.</li>
     </ul></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.5.5ErrorHandlingWhereMultip=
leClassificationsExistAsaResultOfDuplicateClassificationsProvidedByAlation"=
>8.5.5 Error Handling Where Multiple Classifications Exist As a Result Of D=
uplicate Classifications Provided By Alation</h3>
<p>In the event multiple classifications are provided by Alation as a resul=
t of a field being detected by two different Catalogue Sets that have diffe=
rent classifications for a given field, this will need to be managed. To mi=
tigate and treat this edge case scenario, the following considerations will=
 need to be applied.</p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 80=
.3755%;">
  <colgroup>
   <col style=3D"width: 20.4329%;">
   <col style=3D"width: 79.5719%;">
  </colgroup>
  <tbody>
   <tr>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Tool</th>
    <th class=3D"confluenceTh">Mitigation</th>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Alation</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ol style=3D"text-align: left;">
      <li>As Catalogue Sets continue to improve in accuracy and governance =
coverage, the likelihood of conflicting classifications being applied to th=
e same attribute will decrease over time.</li>
      <li>Where a conflict is identified, it will be visible in the Alation=
 UI, allowing data stewards to review and resolve it through standard gover=
nance processes.</li>
      <li>Additionally, a Power BI report can be developed to proactively i=
dentify and alert on catalogue set conflicts. Note: this enhancement is out=
side the current scope of this solution but could be considered as part of =
future data quality monitoring improvements.</li>
     </ol></td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">Snowflake</td>
    <td class=3D"confluenceTd">
     <ol style=3D"text-align: left;">
      <li>In the event of a conflict, and while awaiting resolution, data w=
ill continue to flow into Snowflake as designed.</li>
      <li>To safeguard downstream processes and the masking rules applied, =
the query extracting data from Alation Analytics will need include logic to=
 detect attributes with duplicate or conflicting classifications.</li>
      <li>A ranking rule will be applied during deduplication to apply the =
higher classification of conflict to ensure the appropriate sensitivity lev=
el is used, following this hierarchy:=20
       <ul>
        <li>Ranking 1: Highly Confidential</li>
        <li>Ranking 2: Confidential</li>
        <li>Ranking 3: Private</li>
        <li>Ranking 4: Public</li>
       </ul></li>
     </ol></td>
   </tr>
  </tbody>
 </table>
</div>
<h2 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-RBAC8.6RoleBasedAccessandMask=
ingofData(Components6,7,14,15)"><span class=3D"confluence-anchor-link" id=
=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-RBAC"></span>8.6 Role Based Access =
and Masking of Data (Components 6, 7, 14, 15)</h2>
<h3 id=3D"BDHClassification&amp;MaskingDesign-8.6.1RoleBasedAccessControlBy=
ZonePersonas">8.6.1 Role Based Access Control By Zone Personas</h3>
<p>Access to data within Snowflake is governed through a Role-Based Access =
Control (RBAC) framework that leverages data classification tags to dynamic=
ally enforce access permissions. As described in previous sections, the&nbs=
p;<code>security_classification_code</code> tag defines the sensitivity lev=
el of data (e.g., <em>'Public', 'Private', 'Confidential', 'Highly Confiden=
tial'</em>, or '<em>Unclassified</em>'). A user=E2=80=99s assigned role det=
ermines which classification levels they can view or interact with. The acc=
ompanying RBAC matrix (below) outlines the access permissions applicable fo=
r each persona and environment.</p>
<p><strong><span class=3D"inline-comment-marker" data-ref=3D"a4bd5316-9289-=
4ac8-b84d-c8178ab4d8f8">Landing Zone Role:</span>&nbsp;</strong></p>
<ul>
 <li>The <u>Landing Zone</u> serves as the initial ingestion area and conta=
ins both <u>structured and semi-structured data</u>.</li>
 <li>Only the Landing Developers (as described in the table below - Landing=
 Developers with the '<em>Highly Confidential Role</em>') will have access =
to the Landing Zone and will be to see data nested data structures and/or u=
nclassified data.&nbsp;</li>
 <li>As semi-structured or nested data structures cannot be unpacked by Ala=
tion during initial scans, Alation treats each object as a single column un=
til it is flattened for consumption by downstream layers. These objects by =
default will therefore be considered unclassified and may contain sensitive=
 data. Subsequently, Landing Developers are privileged users with elevated =
access to support ingestion testing and validation activities as part of th=
e initial pipeline build.</li>
 <li>Further, the Landing Zone is not intended as a consumption layer; its =
primary purpose is to enable ingestion teams to land data quickly and valid=
ate ingestion pipelines. Downstream data preparation and unpacking activiti=
es occur within the Subject Area Zone, where data is refined for analytical=
 use. Subsequently, only a Landing Developer should have access to the Land=
ing Zone.&nbsp;</li>
 <li>Given Alation will scan and classify the Landing Zone, additional role=
s are defined to support classification-based access patterns, ensuring pro=
per handling of data across sensitivity levels as the data gets consumed in=
 the subject area Zone.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Service Accounts &amp; Service Roles:</strong></p>
<ul>
 <li>It is important to note that Service Accounts and Service Roles (e.g.,=
 those used by orchestration tools such as Airflow) are not included in thi=
s matrix. These accounts operate under dedicated system identities, and <u>=
no human users</u> have direct access to these roles or credentials. This s=
eparation ensures that automated ingestion and orchestration activities can=
 execute securely and consistently without introducing unnecessary human ac=
cess risk.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Additional Considerations:</strong></p>
<ul>
 <li>As new data products are developed (e.g., new tables or fields), any n=
ewly created data will initially be treated as '<em>Unclassified</em>' and =
will therefore be automatically masked by default. While this approach main=
tains strong data protection, it can temporarily hinder developer productiv=
ity until Alation completes its data scan and applies the appropriate class=
ifications.</li>
 <li>To address this, a&nbsp;Transformation Database (DB)<span style=3D"let=
ter-spacing: 0.0px;"><strong> </strong>is introduced. Within this environme=
nt, data is not automatically set to '</span><em style=3D"letter-spacing: 0=
.0px;">Unclassified'</em><span style=3D"letter-spacing: 0.0px;"> or masked =
for other developer roles, enabling faster development and testing cycles. =
Data security is still maintained, as only the </span>assigned developer<sp=
an style=3D"letter-spacing: 0.0px;"> has access to their designated schema.=
 Further details on this approach are outlined in the subsequent sections.<=
/span></li>
 <li><span style=3D"letter-spacing: 0.0px;"><span class=3D"inline-comment-m=
arker" data-ref=3D"777379bf-193b-4fba-9474-d71fd378d0a8">The Landing Zone i=
s split into two conceptual areas; a General Landing Zone for almost all da=
ta sources and a Restricted Landing Zone where there is heightened concern =
about the sensitivity of the incoming data; such data will be restricted to=
 a small group of extra privileged Landing Developers. To manage this, the =
'Restricted Access' role within the Landing Zone is a special role that res=
tricts and controls access to specific objects within the Landing Zone, whe=
re [RSTSRC] represents the named restriction defined during development; th=
is allows creation of multiple restrictions as the need is required (e.g. C=
ase Investigations, Fraud, Acquisition Data, Distressed Customers, Employee=
 EAP).</span>&nbsp;</span></li>
</ul>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
 <table class=3D"relative-table wrapped confluenceTable" style=3D"width: 88=
